00:19:04.180 --> 00:19:05.980
but that kind of thing does factor in.

528
00:19:05.980 --> 00:19:08.430
If you can safely climb in a,

529
00:19:08.430 --> 00:19:10.200
like basically modern gyms are just safe.

530
00:19:10.200 --> 00:19:11.580
And so you can climb,

531
00:19:11.580 --> 00:19:13.430
you can push yourself super hard physically

532
00:19:13.430 --> 00:19:15.095
with no risk of injury.

533
00:19:15.095 --> 00:19:18.000
When you were doing the "Free Solo" tour,

534
00:19:18.000 --> 00:19:19.540
I mean, it would be, you would share

535
00:19:19.540 --> 00:19:22.760
like in every city that you would go to for the tour.

536
00:19:22.760 --> 00:19:25.339
It was all about like where's the climbing gym

537
00:19:25.339 --> 00:19:26.630
and like the workout that day

538
00:19:26.630 --> 00:19:28.360
before the screening or whatever.

539
00:19:28.360 --> 00:19:30.430
I've sampled like almost every gym in the country.

540
00:19:30.430 --> 00:19:31.860
You must have been in every climbing gym in America.

541
00:19:31.860 --> 00:19:33.060
Yeah, it feels like it,

542
00:19:33.060 --> 00:19:34.780
but actually there's so many gyms in American now

543
00:19:34.780 --> 00:19:37.910
that I've probably sampled a tiny percentage actually.

544
00:19:37.910 --> 00:19:39.840
But it is true that every major city

545
00:19:39.840 --> 00:19:41.994
in the country I've sampled the gym.

546
00:19:41.994 --> 00:19:43.470
'Cause I typically would land.

547
00:19:43.470 --> 00:19:45.810
I would go from the airport straight to the gym.

548
00:19:45.810 --> 00:19:47.200
I would train.

549
00:19:47.200 --> 00:19:48.610
I'd take a shower at the gym.

550
00:19:48.610 --> 00:19:50.300
I would go straight to the venue,

551
00:19:50.300 --> 00:19:51.950
do whatever event I was supposed to do

552
00:19:51.950 --> 00:19:53.490
and then basically either go to a hotel

553
00:19:53.490 --> 00:19:55.080
or go back to the airport.

554
00:19:55.080 --> 00:19:57.660
But it's like, gyms have always felt

555
00:19:57.660 --> 00:19:59.150
like a second home to me almost.

556
00:19:59.150 --> 00:20:00.450
I walk into a climbing gym anywhere

557
00:20:00.450 --> 00:20:02.240
and I'm like, "Ah, home sweet home."

558
00:20:02.240 --> 00:20:03.073
I take your shoes off.

559
00:20:03.073 --> 00:20:04.910
I want around, I dump my stuff everywhere.

560
00:20:04.910 --> 00:20:06.810
But it has to be complicated with you now,

561
00:20:06.810 --> 00:20:09.400
because if you just want to go and get a workout in,

562
00:20:09.400 --> 00:20:11.880
you're gonna have to be a bit of a politician

563
00:20:11.880 --> 00:20:13.052
for a while first.

564
00:20:13.052 --> 00:20:14.610
Depends, depends.

565
00:20:14.610 --> 00:20:15.965
You know, you wear your hood.

566
00:20:15.965 --> 00:20:19.278
You keep your head down and just like climb.

567
00:20:19.278 --> 00:20:21.920
To some extent, it's always a little bit of a scene.

568
00:20:21.920 --> 00:20:25.190
But on the other hand, any gym in the country,

569
00:20:25.190 --> 00:20:27.250
I mean everyone's there to do the same thing as you.

570
00:20:27.250 --> 00:20:29.540
It is nice because you're all just there to climb together.

571
00:20:29.540 --> 00:20:30.857
So when you get past the initial, like,

572
00:20:30.857 --> 00:20:31.727
"Oh, what are you doing here?

573
00:20:31.727 --> 00:20:33.510
"That's crazy. Can I take a picture?"

574
00:20:33.510 --> 00:20:34.343
Ultimately you're like,

575
00:20:34.343 --> 00:20:35.723
"Let's just session all these boulders together."

576
00:20:35.723 --> 00:20:38.160
Because it's like we're all just there to do same thing.

577
00:20:38.160 --> 00:20:40.790
Well, it's been a crazy couple of years for you.

578
00:20:40.790 --> 00:20:43.180
The first time that you did the show,

579
00:20:43.180 --> 00:20:46.660
we talked about the "Free Solo" climb,

580
00:20:46.660 --> 00:20:48.630
but it was before the movie came out.

581
00:20:48.630 --> 00:20:50.420
You remember that?
Yeah, yeah.

582
00:20:50.420 --> 00:20:54.180
So I just remember thinking that I had a grip

583
00:20:54.180 --> 00:20:56.780
on what it is exactly that you had done

584
00:20:56.780 --> 00:21:00.380
and feeling confident that I understood it, (laughs)

585
00:21:00.380 --> 00:21:03.730
and then you invited me to the premiere,

586
00:21:03.730 --> 00:21:05.990
I remember, in Beverly Hills that screening.

587
00:21:05.990 --> 00:21:08.295
And I watched the movie and I was like,

588
00:21:08.295 --> 00:21:09.350
"Oh, I didn't understand it at all."

589
00:21:09.350 --> 00:21:11.530
Like, I'm sure I still don't.

590
00:21:11.530 --> 00:21:14.000
But the movie took it quite a bit further

591
00:21:14.000 --> 00:21:16.270
in terms of getting me to fully grok

592
00:21:16.270 --> 00:21:17.870
what had actually happened.

593
00:21:17.870 --> 00:21:21.165
And then of course, you know, your story's well told

594
00:21:21.165 --> 00:21:23.340
with everything that happened with the movie.

595
00:21:23.340 --> 00:21:25.050
And you went on this crazy press tour

596
00:21:25.050 --> 00:21:27.000
and did eight bazillion interviews.

597
00:21:27.000 --> 00:21:29.846
And I'm sure completely talked out on this movie.

598
00:21:29.846 --> 00:21:33.050
So I wanna talk about other stuff today,

599
00:21:33.050 --> 00:21:37.010
but before we kind of move off of "Free Solo,"

600
00:21:37.010 --> 00:21:39.930
I mean, in the wake of being so exhaustively interviewed

601
00:21:39.930 --> 00:21:43.190
about that, do you feel like there's anything left to say

602
00:21:43.190 --> 00:21:46.440
or anything left unsaid or anything, you know,

603
00:21:46.440 --> 00:21:51.110
looking back on it that you feel like people misunderstood

604
00:21:51.110 --> 00:21:52.430
about the movie or you

605
00:21:52.430 --> 00:21:55.460
or is it just your time for the next chapter?

606
00:21:55.460 --> 00:21:56.767
No, I think it's all pretty well-

607
00:21:56.767 --> 00:21:58.210
It's been said.

608
00:21:58.210 --> 00:21:59.043
Yeah, well, I mean,

609
00:21:59.043 --> 00:22:01.410
I think part of why the film did so well is

610
00:22:01.410 --> 00:22:03.650
that it is an incredibly honest look

611
00:22:03.650 --> 00:22:07.524
at the process leading up to free soloing El Cap.

612
00:22:07.524 --> 00:22:10.710
So I don't know, I've got nothing but positive...

613
00:22:10.710 --> 00:22:14.070
It's like the media tour around it was crazy

614
00:22:14.070 --> 00:22:15.580
and like going to the Oscars was crazy

615
00:22:15.580 --> 00:22:17.543
and meeting Prince William or whatever,

616
00:22:17.543 --> 00:22:18.970
because we won a BAFTA.

617
00:22:18.970 --> 00:22:21.310
So like you're in the UK, like meeting royalty or whatever.

618
00:22:21.310 --> 00:22:23.970
Like it's all totally crazy whirlwind tour,

619
00:22:23.970 --> 00:22:27.550
but I can still look back on it and be happy

620
00:22:27.550 --> 00:22:28.383
about the whole thing, you know?

621
00:22:28.383 --> 00:22:32.070
'Cause I am, you know, understandably proud

622
00:22:32.070 --> 00:22:33.160
of free soloing El Cap.

623
00:22:33.160 --> 00:22:34.520
It was something very difficult

624
00:22:34.520 --> 00:22:36.690
that I worked hard for, that I'm proud of.

625
00:22:36.690 --> 00:22:39.255
And I think the film reflected that really well

626
00:22:39.255 --> 00:22:41.070
and very honestly.

627
00:22:41.070 --> 00:22:42.680
And so I'm like proud of the film,

628
00:22:42.680 --> 00:22:44.049
I'm proud of the whole deal.

629
00:22:44.049 --> 00:22:44.989
It's like, it's cool.

630
00:22:44.989 --> 00:22:46.233
It was a phenomenon though.

631
00:22:46.233 --> 00:22:48.320
I mean, everybody was talking about this movie.

632
00:22:48.320 --> 00:22:50.960
For a documentary to break out like that

633
00:22:50.960 --> 00:22:52.379
was just extraordinary.

634
00:22:52.379 --> 00:22:54.970
Yeah, I mean, I don't, you know,

635
00:22:54.970 --> 00:22:57.531
I was like so in it that I can't really say.

636
00:22:57.531 --> 00:22:59.467
I was just surviving the tour.

637
00:22:59.467 --> 00:23:01.312
It was like such a whirlwind.

638
00:23:01.312 --> 00:23:03.353
But no, now looking back on it

639
00:23:03.353 --> 00:23:05.393
with a little bit of perspective a few years away,

640
00:23:05.393 --> 00:23:07.647
I'm like, "What a life experience,

641
00:23:07.647 --> 00:23:09.110
"what a crazy whirlwind."

642
00:23:09.110 --> 00:23:10.490
Crazy, man.

643
00:23:10.490 --> 00:23:12.740
I mean, before the movie was made,

644
00:23:12.740 --> 00:23:16.200
I joked that all I wanted was to see El Cap on IMAX,

645
00:23:16.200 --> 00:23:18.840
just because El Cap to me is the most meaningful wall

646
00:23:18.840 --> 00:23:19.673
in the world.

647
00:23:19.673 --> 00:23:21.870
It's like the most beautiful, iconic face.

648
00:23:21.870 --> 00:23:23.870
And to see it on the biggest possible screen,

649
00:23:23.870 --> 00:23:25.050
it was like, that's cool.

650
00:23:25.050 --> 00:23:28.370
And you know, sure enough, "Free Solo" showed on IMAX

651
00:23:28.370 --> 00:23:30.670
and I got to see the movie on IMAX a couple of times.

652
00:23:30.670 --> 00:23:32.027
And I was like, "This is so awesome."

653
00:23:32.027 --> 00:23:32.860
(laughs) You love it.

654
00:23:32.860 --> 00:23:33.790
You know, it's just, it's cool.

655
00:23:33.790 --> 00:23:35.770
I was like, oh, we did the wall justice.

656
00:23:35.770 --> 00:23:37.940
We did, you know, we did Yosemite justice.

657
00:23:37.940 --> 00:23:39.100
It was like, I'm pretty proud of that.

658
00:23:39.100 --> 00:23:41.930
And you did the world of climbing justice.

659
00:23:41.930 --> 00:23:44.300
I mean, it was so effective

660
00:23:44.300 --> 00:23:48.649
in introducing the magnitude of what you do to the world

661
00:23:48.649 --> 00:23:53.649
and what's so kind of cherished about the community.

662
00:23:56.160 --> 00:23:59.040
Or maybe too effective because then everyone

663
00:23:59.040 --> 00:24:01.107
that doesn't know anything about climbing, they're like,

664
00:24:01.107 --> 00:24:02.753
"So do you use a rope?"

665
00:24:02.753 --> 00:24:03.877
And you're like, "Yeah, mostly use of rope.

666
00:24:03.877 --> 00:24:06.190
"It's just this one crazy movie like happened to,"

667
00:24:06.190 --> 00:24:08.461
'cause I know a lot of my serious climbing friends,

668
00:24:08.461 --> 00:24:10.567
their families watched the movie and then they're like,

669
00:24:10.567 --> 00:24:12.315
"Is that what you're doing?"

670
00:24:12.315 --> 00:24:13.260
And they're like, "No, no, that's not what I'm doing."

671
00:24:13.260 --> 00:24:16.620
And that's not even what I'm mostly doing,

672
00:24:16.620 --> 00:24:18.530
because I'm typically climbing with partners

673
00:24:18.530 --> 00:24:22.054
and training and just like climbing normally.

674
00:24:22.054 --> 00:24:24.650
But obviously the documentary is focused entirely

675
00:24:24.650 --> 00:24:28.891
on this, you know, this one sort of quixotic goal.

676
00:24:28.891 --> 00:24:30.850
But you know, even that,

677
00:24:30.850 --> 00:24:33.780
I mean, they filmed for two years, you know,

678
00:24:33.780 --> 00:24:34.810
the preparation training

679
00:24:34.810 --> 00:24:37.041
and like getting ready for that climb.

680
00:24:37.041 --> 00:24:41.470
And I did maybe a half dozen sort of cutting edge free solos

681
00:24:41.470 --> 00:24:42.303
to build up to that.

682
00:24:42.303 --> 00:24:44.010
But still that's like seven days of soloing

683
00:24:44.010 --> 00:24:45.900
in the two years that we were filming.

684
00:24:45.900 --> 00:24:46.877
So you watch the film and you're like,

685
00:24:46.877 --> 00:24:47.860
"He's a crazy soloist."

686
00:24:47.860 --> 00:24:51.700
And you're like, "Yeah, for seven days out of two years."

687
00:24:51.700 --> 00:24:54.190
You gotta keep it in perspective.

688
00:24:54.190 --> 00:24:59.190
So you go from being, living a certain kind of lifestyle

689
00:24:59.530 --> 00:25:00.830
to be the climber that you are.

690
00:25:00.830 --> 00:25:02.380
And then you have this whirlwind crazy

691
00:25:02.380 --> 00:25:03.370
like Hollywood year

692
00:25:03.370 --> 00:25:06.490
where you're just on planes and going,

693
00:25:06.490 --> 00:25:08.150
I mean, your life is upside down.

694
00:25:08.150 --> 00:25:12.610
So I would suspect that the pandemic

695
00:25:12.610 --> 00:25:14.930
for better or worse has kind of suited you

696
00:25:14.930 --> 00:25:17.370
to like kind of get back to just doing what you do

697
00:25:17.370 --> 00:25:18.560
and like being left alone,

698
00:25:18.560 --> 00:25:20.670
so you can just climb and live your life.

699
00:25:20.670 --> 00:25:22.213
Yeah, it has.

700
00:25:23.290 --> 00:25:25.680
Yeah, I mean, so I live in Las Vegas.

701
00:25:25.680 --> 00:25:27.787
And through the whole pandemic,

702
00:25:27.787 --> 00:25:31.080
even during like lockdowns and shutdowns and everything,

703
00:25:31.080 --> 00:25:33.180
outdoor recreation was always explicitly allowed

704
00:25:33.180 --> 00:25:34.660
in the state of Nevada.

705
00:25:34.660 --> 00:25:36.760
So combined with the fact

706
00:25:36.760 --> 00:25:38.510
that there's almost limitless rock around town,

707
00:25:38.510 --> 00:25:40.630
it was always totally okay for us to go out

708
00:25:40.630 --> 00:25:41.860
and develop some new climbing areas

709
00:25:41.860 --> 00:25:44.170
and go climbing and do things near the house.

710
00:25:44.170 --> 00:25:46.142
And so it was an incredible place to live.

711
00:25:46.142 --> 00:25:48.110
Yeah, I mean the whole pandemic

712
00:25:48.110 --> 00:25:50.020
for me has just been sort of returning to roots.

713
00:25:50.020 --> 00:25:51.540
You know, I have no obligations.

714
00:25:51.540 --> 00:25:52.640
I go climb every day.

715
00:25:52.640 --> 00:25:55.320
I explore new crags, you know, develop new climbs.

716
00:25:55.320 --> 00:25:57.420
But it's like, oh, you know, it's not that bad.

717
00:25:57.420 --> 00:26:00.340
And I know that's an incredibly fortunate position to be in.

718
00:26:00.340 --> 00:26:02.303
Though it's also, I mean, I do live there for a reason.

719
00:26:02.303 --> 00:26:04.830
It's like, I moved there because it's the best rock

720
00:26:04.830 --> 00:26:07.600
and because of that degree of access.

721
00:26:07.600 --> 00:26:08.780
And you're married now.

722
00:26:08.780 --> 00:26:09.613
I am married now.

723
00:26:09.613 --> 00:26:11.270
Yeah, that also happened during the pandemic.

724
00:26:11.270 --> 00:26:12.660
But that's the thing is, I mean,

725
00:26:12.660 --> 00:26:14.507
personally it's been kind of a good year.

726
00:26:14.507 --> 00:26:18.530
But it's like embarrassing to say so,

727
00:26:18.530 --> 00:26:20.100
because obviously there's been so much hardship

728
00:26:20.100 --> 00:26:21.250
for so many other people.

729
00:26:21.250 --> 00:26:22.920
But you know, sometimes things just work out in life,

730
00:26:22.920 --> 00:26:25.312
and you're like, nothing wrong with it.

731
00:26:25.312 --> 00:26:26.180
So how are you settling in?

732
00:26:26.180 --> 00:26:27.837
How's Sanni doing?

733
00:26:27.837 --> 00:26:29.140
No, Sanni's good.

734
00:26:29.140 --> 00:26:29.973
Sanni is actually great.

735
00:26:29.973 --> 00:26:33.070
Sanni is in a weird, like, I don't know

736
00:26:33.070 --> 00:26:34.890
if podcasts listeners care about this kind of thing,

737
00:26:34.890 --> 00:26:36.560
but she's like climbing really hard right now,

738
00:26:36.560 --> 00:26:38.530
which has started this whole interesting

739
00:26:38.530 --> 00:26:41.040
positive feedback thing where because she's really strong,

740
00:26:41.040 --> 00:26:42.440
she's like more excited about it.

741
00:26:42.440 --> 00:26:43.865
Because it's like more fun for her,

742
00:26:43.865 --> 00:26:45.840
which makes her more excited to train harder,

743
00:26:45.840 --> 00:26:46.890
which is making her stronger.

744
00:26:46.890 --> 00:26:49.740
She's like in this total positive phase of life right now

745
00:26:49.740 --> 00:26:50.870
where she's like really cranking.

746
00:26:50.870 --> 00:26:52.230
She's like climbing her hardest grades.

747
00:26:52.230 --> 00:26:54.430
She's all fired up.
That's cool.

748
00:26:54.430 --> 00:26:56.030
Yeah, we just trained in the gym last night,

749
00:26:56.030 --> 00:26:59.930
and she was like performing to a degree

750
00:26:59.930 --> 00:27:01.390
that I was like, "Huh."

751
00:27:01.390 --> 00:27:02.570
It's like damn.

752
00:27:02.570 --> 00:27:04.360
You know, because I've been gone on expedition for a month,

753
00:27:04.360 --> 00:27:05.930
and she's been basically just like cranking

754
00:27:05.930 --> 00:27:07.220
for the month I was gone.

755
00:27:07.220 --> 00:27:09.430
And I came home and I'm like, "Whoa."

756
00:27:09.430 --> 00:27:11.700
Was she even a climber when you guys first met?

757
00:27:11.700 --> 00:27:14.087
She had barely started climbing when we first met.

758
00:27:14.087 --> 00:27:17.280
Her sister was into it and her cousin to some extent,

759
00:27:17.280 --> 00:27:19.760
basically like her family got her into it a tiny bit.

760
00:27:19.760 --> 00:27:21.380
And then when we started dating,

761
00:27:21.380 --> 00:27:22.990
obviously she started climbing more.

762
00:27:22.990 --> 00:27:24.820
Yeah, that's good.

763
00:27:24.820 --> 00:27:26.686
Yeah, now she's like pretty good.

764
00:27:26.686 --> 00:27:27.736
She's super into it.

765
00:27:27.736 --> 00:27:28.569
Well, it's just like anything,

766
00:27:28.569 --> 00:27:29.980
you get good at it and you get strong

767
00:27:29.980 --> 00:27:32.210
and then you're more emotionally connected to it.

768
00:27:32.210 --> 00:27:33.450
Yeah, of course.
You're more excited.

769
00:27:33.450 --> 00:27:35.473
And I think for her,

770
00:27:37.090 --> 00:27:38.320
you know, I don't know for sure,

771
00:27:38.320 --> 00:27:40.040
but I think as she started climbing,

772
00:27:40.040 --> 00:27:41.670
there was a little more fear involved.

773
00:27:41.670 --> 00:27:44.100
You know, 'cause climbing is kind of serious

774
00:27:44.100 --> 00:27:45.070
and kind of hardcore.

775
00:27:45.070 --> 00:27:47.200
And I think that because she was dating me,

776
00:27:47.200 --> 00:27:48.960
she kind of like went into the deep end right away

777
00:27:48.960 --> 00:27:51.340
and was maybe exposed to like a little too much

778
00:27:51.340 --> 00:27:54.360
of the like hardcore side of climbing straight away.

779
00:27:54.360 --> 00:27:56.090
And I think now as she gets like stronger

780
00:27:56.090 --> 00:27:59.520
and more able and can like do more as a climber,

781
00:27:59.520 --> 00:28:01.410
it's all like much less scary.

782
00:28:01.410 --> 00:28:03.010
And so it's like obviously more fun

783
00:28:03.010 --> 00:28:04.380
when you're not scared at all.

784
00:28:04.380 --> 00:28:06.250
It's like she's doing it in reverse.

785
00:28:06.250 --> 00:28:08.627
Let's start with free soloing El Cap,

786
00:28:08.627 --> 00:28:10.950
and we'll work our way backwards to the climbing gym.

787
00:28:10.950 --> 00:28:12.850
Yeah. No, I mean, it kind of is.

788
00:28:12.850 --> 00:28:15.710
Certainly psychologically I think that's right.

789
00:28:15.710 --> 00:28:16.920
That's funny.

790
00:28:16.920 --> 00:28:20.050
And there's some degree of luck involved

791
00:28:20.050 --> 00:28:22.340
with it all too, because like, because she's been connected

792
00:28:22.340 --> 00:28:25.120
with me for years and I know like basically everybody

793
00:28:25.120 --> 00:28:26.670
in the climbing community,

794
00:28:26.670 --> 00:28:28.510
over the last few years there were sort of

795
00:28:28.510 --> 00:28:30.570
like a series of unfortunate accidents

796
00:28:30.570 --> 00:28:34.050
where like people, prominent climbers died in various ways.

797
00:28:34.050 --> 00:28:37.350
And so, you know, all of those sorts of accidents

798
00:28:37.350 --> 00:28:38.860
like affected her personally in a way

799
00:28:38.860 --> 00:28:41.050
that had she just started climbing in the gym

800
00:28:41.050 --> 00:28:43.062
and not known me, she wouldn't know any of the people

801
00:28:43.062 --> 00:28:45.260
and it wouldn't have like touched her quite as much.

802
00:28:45.260 --> 00:28:46.093
I don't know.

803
00:28:46.093 --> 00:28:47.944
That's the kind of thing that you're like,

804
00:28:47.944 --> 00:28:48.800
you know, sorry to drag her into it.

805
00:28:48.800 --> 00:28:50.707
This is the world you're in though.

806
00:28:50.707 --> 00:28:51.540
Yeah.

807
00:28:51.540 --> 00:28:53.300
Well, but there is a degree of luck to that,

808
00:28:53.300 --> 00:28:56.010
because for several years it seemed like really bad

809
00:28:56.010 --> 00:28:57.240
where like there were just a bunch

810
00:28:57.240 --> 00:28:59.500
of high profile accidents that were kind of terrible.

811
00:28:59.500 --> 00:29:00.730
But then you can go five years

812
00:29:00.730 --> 00:29:02.483
and have nobody that you know die.

813
00:29:03.563 --> 00:29:04.720
I don't know.

814
00:29:04.720 --> 00:29:06.710
Yeah, but it's one of the few sports

815
00:29:06.710 --> 00:29:09.720
where like what you just said would even come up.

816
00:29:09.720 --> 00:29:10.553
Yeah, well, I don't know.

817
00:29:10.553 --> 00:29:11.910
If you're road biking all the time, people-

818
00:29:11.910 --> 00:29:12.930
Yeah, that's true.

819
00:29:12.930 --> 00:29:13.763
That's true.

820
00:29:13.763 --> 00:29:15.550
There was just a terrible road biking thing in Vegas

821
00:29:15.550 --> 00:29:17.110
that like totally shook the cycling community.

822
00:29:17.110 --> 00:29:17.943
Oh, wow. I didn't know that.

823
00:29:17.943 --> 00:29:20.780
It was like a truck basically

824
00:29:20.780 --> 00:29:22.510
like drove over like seven cyclists

825
00:29:22.510 --> 00:29:24.377
on a group run and killed six of them or something.

826
00:29:24.377 --> 00:29:25.620
It was like crazy.

827
00:29:25.620 --> 00:29:27.400
Oh man. I hate hearing that.

828
00:29:27.400 --> 00:29:28.233
I know.

829
00:29:28.233 --> 00:29:29.310
That's the thing is I'm always like, "Oh."

830
00:29:29.310 --> 00:29:30.410
And you're out on your bike.

831
00:29:30.410 --> 00:29:31.550
I've noticed that you've done,

832
00:29:31.550 --> 00:29:34.240
like during the pandemic, you kind of have created,

833
00:29:34.240 --> 00:29:35.073
we're gonna talk about Guyana,

834
00:29:35.073 --> 00:29:35.906
but you've kind of created

835
00:29:35.906 --> 00:29:37.540
these endurance challenges for yourself,

836
00:29:37.540 --> 00:29:39.450
like I'm gonna ride my bike 100 miles

837
00:29:39.450 --> 00:29:41.830
and then climb this mountain and ride home in the dark.

838
00:29:41.830 --> 00:29:43.575
Yeah, that sums it up.

839
00:29:43.575 --> 00:29:44.408
(Rich laughs)

840
00:29:44.408 --> 00:29:46.230
Well, you know, because if you live in one place

841
00:29:46.230 --> 00:29:47.870
and you don't have access to big mountains,

842
00:29:47.870 --> 00:29:48.703
you can at least make the mountains

843
00:29:48.703 --> 00:29:51.288
that you do have challenging in an appropriate way.

844
00:29:51.288 --> 00:29:54.590
I'm gonna show up exhausted and see how I do when-

845
00:29:54.590 --> 00:29:57.570
Well, I really love challenges that you can do from home

846
00:29:57.570 --> 00:29:58.960
when you just bike out of your driveway

847
00:29:58.960 --> 00:30:00.830
and then have this crazy epic day in the mountains,

848
00:30:00.830 --> 00:30:02.810
then bike back to your home.

849
00:30:02.810 --> 00:30:05.000
And you know, to be able to do that in the suburbs

850
00:30:05.000 --> 00:30:06.380
in Las Vegas is pretty awesome.

851
00:30:06.380 --> 00:30:08.810
You know, you're like biking along surface streets

852
00:30:08.810 --> 00:30:10.160
just like, and especially in Vegas,

853
00:30:10.160 --> 00:30:11.760
I actually ride on the sidewalk a lot,

854
00:30:11.760 --> 00:30:12.650
which is kind of embarrassing,

855
00:30:12.650 --> 00:30:14.860
but it's the best way to not get hit by a car.

856
00:30:14.860 --> 00:30:15.873
I feel like a little kid or something

857
00:30:15.873 --> 00:30:17.950
who's like afraid to bike in the shoulder.

858
00:30:17.950 --> 00:30:19.530
But there are big sections of Vegas

859
00:30:19.530 --> 00:30:21.700
where it's definitely safer to ride the sidewalk.

860
00:30:21.700 --> 00:30:24.090
And you know, you're like, "This is totally absurd,"

861
00:30:24.090 --> 00:30:25.520
but then three hours later,

862
00:30:25.520 --> 00:30:28.500
you're grinding up this crazy hill, like a mountainside.

863
00:30:28.500 --> 00:30:29.630
And then a couple hours after that,

864
00:30:29.630 --> 00:30:32.240
you're like hiking through the woods by yourself.

865
00:30:32.240 --> 00:30:34.430
And it's pretty amazing to have that kind of access.

866
00:30:34.430 --> 00:30:36.670
So let's talk about Guyana.

867
00:30:36.670 --> 00:30:39.090
You were like off the grid for like a month.

868
00:30:39.090 --> 00:30:40.930
It's like this Nat Geo project.

869
00:30:40.930 --> 00:30:42.070
Can you talk about that or?

870
00:30:42.070 --> 00:30:42.903
I think so.

871
00:30:42.903 --> 00:30:44.790
I mean, who's gonna stop me?
I don't know.

872
00:30:44.790 --> 00:30:45.623
I'm not. (laughs)

873
00:30:45.623 --> 00:30:46.456
Yeah, exactly.

874
00:30:46.456 --> 00:30:47.768
No, no, I think it's all totally fine.

875
00:30:47.768 --> 00:30:49.230
There's no network bosses that's gonna call here

876
00:30:49.230 --> 00:30:50.077
and tell you to shut up.

877
00:30:50.077 --> 00:30:50.910
Yeah.

878
00:30:50.910 --> 00:30:52.000
No, I think it's all fine.

879
00:30:52.000 --> 00:30:54.680
Yeah, so it was being filmed for an episode

880
00:30:54.680 --> 00:30:59.294
of National Geographic Explorer, which I think is a series.

881
00:30:59.294 --> 00:31:01.494
It was being filmed by some friends of mine.

882
00:31:02.800 --> 00:31:04.060
And it was a crazy trip.

883
00:31:04.060 --> 00:31:07.060
Yeah, so we flew into Guyana into Georgetown.

884
00:31:07.060 --> 00:31:10.490
And then the goal was to climb this tepui,

885
00:31:10.490 --> 00:31:11.710
which is like a big sandstone,

886
00:31:11.710 --> 00:31:13.300
well, sort of quartzitic sandstone wall

887
00:31:13.300 --> 00:31:14.851
that sticks out of the jungle.

888
00:31:14.851 --> 00:31:16.320
And there are a bunch of tepuis

889
00:31:16.320 --> 00:31:18.120
that are kind of scattered across Venezuela

890
00:31:18.120 --> 00:31:19.600
and Guyana and Brazil.

891
00:31:19.600 --> 00:31:22.030
Actually, have you seen the movie "Up"?

892
00:31:22.030 --> 00:31:23.900
The really like charming Disney movie,

893
00:31:23.900 --> 00:31:24.949
Pixar movie or whatever?

894
00:31:24.949 --> 00:31:27.689
With like floating balloon house like over the...

895
00:31:27.689 --> 00:31:29.330
Those are tepuis.

896
00:31:29.330 --> 00:31:30.430
So that's like what we were trying to climb.

897
00:31:30.430 --> 00:31:31.700
Wow.

898
00:31:31.700 --> 00:31:33.030
or if you've seen the new "Point Break,"

899
00:31:33.030 --> 00:31:34.546
that was also filmed in tepuis.

900
00:31:34.546 --> 00:31:37.417
There's something like that in "Avatar" too.

901
00:31:37.417 --> 00:31:39.670
No, the ones in "Avatar" are modeled

902
00:31:39.670 --> 00:31:40.660
on this area in China.

903
00:31:40.660 --> 00:31:41.848
That's like limestone.

904
00:31:41.848 --> 00:31:46.848
It's like a different, it's caused in a different way.

905
00:31:47.070 --> 00:31:48.570
Like it's a different geological process

906
00:31:48.570 --> 00:31:50.290
that creates the "Avatar" mountains.

907
00:31:50.290 --> 00:31:53.410
But yeah, it is kind of a similarly surreal

908
00:31:53.410 --> 00:31:57.007
mountain landscape with dense jungle around it.

909
00:31:57.007 --> 00:31:59.490
But anyway, so we were going to climb this wall

910
00:31:59.490 --> 00:32:00.323
that hadn't been climbed.

911
00:32:00.323 --> 00:32:02.460
And then we also had this sort

912
00:32:02.460 --> 00:32:03.850
of renowned biologist with us,

913
00:32:03.850 --> 00:32:07.656
this guy Bruce Means who had done extensive work

914
00:32:07.656 --> 00:32:11.000
researching the frog species in the area.

915
00:32:11.000 --> 00:32:14.510
So he was gonna sort of finish this transect

916
00:32:14.510 --> 00:32:19.110
of the river basin that this wall formed the top of.

917
00:32:19.110 --> 00:32:22.280
And he was basically researching different species

918
00:32:22.280 --> 00:32:23.600
of frog along the way.

919
00:32:23.600 --> 00:32:25.410
And so it was kind of a combined trip

920
00:32:25.410 --> 00:32:26.800
where we were gonna get him up the wall

921
00:32:26.800 --> 00:32:29.250
so that he could find some of the frogs on the summit

922
00:32:29.250 --> 00:32:31.030
and possibly on the cliff itself.

923
00:32:31.030 --> 00:32:33.384
So it's like adventure meets science experiment?

924
00:32:33.384 --> 00:32:34.217
Yeah, adventure meets science.

925
00:32:34.217 --> 00:32:35.723
Which is sort of the perfect Nat Geo thing,

926
00:32:35.723 --> 00:32:37.280
where it's like adventurous

927
00:32:37.280 --> 00:32:41.000
but there's a strong educational scientific component to it.

928
00:32:41.000 --> 00:32:44.770
And why had those peaks not been climbed before?

929
00:32:44.770 --> 00:32:48.340
So that particular peak hadn't been climbed,

930
00:32:48.340 --> 00:32:50.850
I think largely because it's next to this mountain

931
00:32:50.850 --> 00:32:53.660
called Roraima, which is, I forget what the name means,

932
00:32:53.660 --> 00:32:55.770
but it's like source of waters or something.

933
00:32:55.770 --> 00:32:58.860
Roraima is the, it marks the boundary

934
00:32:58.860 --> 00:33:00.840
between Venezuela, Guyana and Brazil.

935
00:33:00.840 --> 00:33:03.430
So like this one point in the middle of this big mountain,

936
00:33:03.430 --> 00:33:05.010
it's the highest mountain in the region.

937
00:33:05.010 --> 00:33:07.299
And then from that summit,

938
00:33:07.299 --> 00:33:09.890
the water that comes off the various sides,

939
00:33:09.890 --> 00:33:12.330
like one aspect drains into the Amazon River,

940
00:33:12.330 --> 00:33:14.947
one drains into the Orinoco in Venezuela, I think,

941
00:33:14.947 --> 00:33:17.190
and the other into Guyana.

942
00:33:17.190 --> 00:33:18.540
But so basically it's a, you know,

943
00:33:18.540 --> 00:33:20.950
imagine a big mountain that splits three countries

944
00:33:20.950 --> 00:33:24.220
and splits the headwaters of three distinct basins.

945
00:33:24.220 --> 00:33:27.270
So it's like this really famous sort of important peak.

946
00:33:27.270 --> 00:33:29.150
And we were climbing the little peak next to it.

947
00:33:29.150 --> 00:33:31.290
So you can kind of see why the little peak next to it

948
00:33:31.290 --> 00:33:33.080
hadn't really had much action over the years.

949
00:33:33.080 --> 00:33:34.920
Yeah, 'cause like people love Roraima

950
00:33:34.920 --> 00:33:37.610
and Roraima has this huge history to it.

951
00:33:37.610 --> 00:33:39.990
And like people were doing expeditions there in 1800s.

952
00:33:39.990 --> 00:33:42.070
People are all about Roraima.

953
00:33:42.070 --> 00:33:43.460
Nobody cares about Wei-Assipu,

954
00:33:43.460 --> 00:33:45.900
which is the little peak next to it that we were doing.

955
00:33:45.900 --> 00:33:46.960
And one of the things, I mean,

956
00:33:46.960 --> 00:33:48.630
based on what you were sharing on social media

957
00:33:48.630 --> 00:33:52.020
was just like how wet it was and just the condensation.

958
00:33:52.020 --> 00:33:53.904
Yeah, I mean, technically we were there in the dry season,

959
00:33:53.904 --> 00:33:56.080
but it rained something like eight hours a day.

960
00:33:56.080 --> 00:33:57.400
It was like totally insane.

961
00:33:57.400 --> 00:33:58.400
Every day we would joke.

962
00:33:58.400 --> 00:34:00.200
We're like, "What the heck kind of dry season is this?"

963
00:34:00.200 --> 00:34:01.800
It's like so crazy.

964
00:34:01.800 --> 00:34:05.285
But technically was it a difficult climb?

965
00:34:05.285 --> 00:34:06.440
Or it just hadn't been done?

966
00:34:06.440 --> 00:34:07.273
It hadn't been done.

967
00:34:07.273 --> 00:34:08.230
I mean, we didn't know, obviously,

968
00:34:08.230 --> 00:34:09.063
'cause it'd never been done.

969
00:34:09.063 --> 00:34:13.214
So we climbed this sort of large overhanging wall.

970
00:34:13.214 --> 00:34:15.440
You kind of have to only climb the overhangs there,

971
00:34:15.440 --> 00:34:16.450
because otherwise they're covered

972
00:34:16.450 --> 00:34:19.320
in vegetation and water, which is cool.

973
00:34:19.320 --> 00:34:21.010
It makes the style of the climbing really fun.

974
00:34:21.010 --> 00:34:22.476
The rock is incredible.

975
00:34:22.476 --> 00:34:23.309
It's some of the best rock on Earth,

976
00:34:23.309 --> 00:34:24.470
it's like this amazing quartzite,

977
00:34:24.470 --> 00:34:26.160
really hard, really solid.

978
00:34:26.160 --> 00:34:29.710
And then what we did for climbers is a six-pitch, 12B.

979
00:34:29.710 --> 00:34:31.790
So it's like, you know, hard enough

980
00:34:31.790 --> 00:34:35.230
that it's like it's not easy by any means,

981
00:34:35.230 --> 00:34:37.942
but it's not like cutting edge elite climbing either.

982
00:34:37.942 --> 00:34:40.161
But for an expedition like that, it's pretty,

983
00:34:40.161 --> 00:34:41.900
you know, it's solid.

984
00:34:41.900 --> 00:34:43.490
We did something that we're proud of.

985
00:34:43.490 --> 00:34:44.660
Well, you were there a month.

986
00:34:44.660 --> 00:34:46.672
Did he find his frogs?
Yeah, he did.

987
00:34:46.672 --> 00:34:47.918
Did he get the frogs sorted out?

988
00:34:47.918 --> 00:34:49.600
Yeah.

989
00:34:49.600 --> 00:34:50.763
So Bruce,

990
00:34:51.672 --> 00:34:54.493
so actually he probably doesn't want too much said about it,

991
00:34:54.493 --> 00:34:55.670
because I think he's gonna publish it all

992
00:34:55.670 --> 00:34:56.920
in scientific journals and things.

993
00:34:56.920 --> 00:35:00.310
But basically he left the jungle with many, many specimens.

994
00:35:00.310 --> 00:35:03.263
Like he had glass jars full of specimens.

995
00:35:03.263 --> 00:35:06.170
And then he'll take them back to his university

996
00:35:06.170 --> 00:35:08.320
and do DNA sequencing on all of them

997
00:35:08.320 --> 00:35:09.880
to see whether or not they're new species,

998
00:35:09.880 --> 00:35:12.950
whether or not they're related to existing things,

999
00:35:12.950 --> 00:35:14.530
just basically to break it all down.

1000
00:35:14.530 --> 00:35:17.530
But he was personally focused on the frogs,

1001
00:35:17.530 --> 00:35:20.850
but he also took many other creepy crawlies of interest.

1002
00:35:20.850 --> 00:35:22.458
'Cause basically anything,

1003
00:35:22.458 --> 00:35:24.360
the thing with where we were

1004
00:35:24.360 --> 00:35:25.660
is that no one's ever been there before.

1005
00:35:25.660 --> 00:35:27.617
So there's been like no science done,

1006
00:35:27.617 --> 00:35:30.480
because there's the rainforest approach

1007
00:35:30.480 --> 00:35:31.650
to get into the mountains.

1008
00:35:31.650 --> 00:35:34.060
But then between the rainforest and the actual mountain,

1009
00:35:34.060 --> 00:35:36.340
which is say three or 4,000 feet higher,

1010
00:35:36.340 --> 00:35:38.090
there's a big, steep, long hillside

1011
00:35:38.090 --> 00:35:40.051
which is sort of the cloud forest,

1012
00:35:40.051 --> 00:35:42.470
which is like if you imagine like stunted

1013
00:35:42.470 --> 00:35:45.270
kind of gnarly trees growing over the talus field,

1014
00:35:45.270 --> 00:35:47.658
like the rocks that would have been below the cliff,

1015
00:35:47.658 --> 00:35:50.110
it's like a whole different ecosystem.

1016
00:35:50.110 --> 00:35:53.030
And so no one had ever been through the cloud forest at all

1017
00:35:53.030 --> 00:35:54.040
in this area.

1018
00:35:54.040 --> 00:35:58.270
So potentially anything that he found there alive

1019
00:35:58.270 --> 00:35:59.260
could be new to science.

1020
00:35:59.260 --> 00:36:02.700
So, you know, I think he cast a pretty wide net

1021
00:36:02.700 --> 00:36:04.330
in terms of collecting specimens,

1022
00:36:04.330 --> 00:36:05.860
because like anything could be new

1023
00:36:05.860 --> 00:36:08.720
and it could all contribute to scientists' understanding

1024
00:36:08.720 --> 00:36:09.740
of that biome.

1025
00:36:09.740 --> 00:36:10.573
Wow.

1026
00:36:10.573 --> 00:36:12.420
So did you have to like bushwhack in

1027
00:36:12.420 --> 00:36:14.600
and were you camping out in there the whole time?

1028
00:36:14.600 --> 00:36:17.290
How difficult was it to actually just to even get in there?

1029
00:36:17.290 --> 00:36:19.300
Dude, bushwhacking does not begin

1030
00:36:19.300 --> 00:36:20.560
to describe the whole...

1031
00:36:21.720 --> 00:36:24.300
The cloud forest, we called it the slime forest,

1032
00:36:24.300 --> 00:36:29.300
because it's like, it was really interesting.

1033
00:36:29.310 --> 00:36:30.250
I read a couple of books

1034
00:36:30.250 --> 00:36:31.790
about sort of natural history of Guyana

1035
00:36:31.790 --> 00:36:34.620
while we were there, 'cause you know, it's the tropics.

1036
00:36:34.620 --> 00:36:36.930
So it's dark from 6:00 p.m. to 6:00 a.m. every day.

1037
00:36:36.930 --> 00:36:39.020
And we were staying in hammocks the whole time.

1038
00:36:39.020 --> 00:36:40.590
There's nothing like, there's no flat ground.

1039
00:36:40.590 --> 00:36:42.400
Basically the whole trip, there's no flat ground.

1040
00:36:42.400 --> 00:36:44.700
So you're always just rigging your hammock between trees.

1041
00:36:44.700 --> 00:36:46.760
And so I was in my hammock for like 12 hours a day,

1042
00:36:46.760 --> 00:36:47.670
