00:46:05.610 --> 00:46:09.410
Yeah, that's one of the projects I'm most proud of

1297
00:46:09.410 --> 00:46:10.243
through the Honnold Foundation,

1298
00:46:10.243 --> 00:46:11.580
because it's just such an interesting

1299
00:46:11.580 --> 00:46:14.210
and potentially transformative project.

1300
00:46:14.210 --> 00:46:17.170
But so just for context it's in Adjuntas in Puerto Rico,

1301
00:46:17.170 --> 00:46:18.700
which is kind of the center of Puerto Rico,

1302
00:46:18.700 --> 00:46:21.092
which was pretty hard hit by the hurricane.

1303
00:46:21.092 --> 00:46:23.640
And basically, because it's sort of in the center

1304
00:46:23.640 --> 00:46:26.280
of the island, it was cut off, you know,

1305
00:46:26.280 --> 00:46:27.790
transmission distribution lines for power

1306
00:46:27.790 --> 00:46:29.110
were sort of severed for a long time.

1307
00:46:29.110 --> 00:46:31.810
So you wound up with the whole city off the grid

1308
00:46:31.810 --> 00:46:33.950
for like months after the hurricane.

1309
00:46:33.950 --> 00:46:36.140
And so sort of just as luck would have it,

1310
00:46:36.140 --> 00:46:40.560
that community has this community organization

1311
00:46:40.560 --> 00:46:42.780
called Casa Pueblo, which has already done

1312
00:46:42.780 --> 00:46:44.840
sort of environmentally-focused community organizing

1313
00:46:44.840 --> 00:46:47.100
for many years, like opposing a big mine

1314
00:46:47.100 --> 00:46:48.640
in the middle of the country and a couple other

1315
00:46:48.640 --> 00:46:51.520
sort of environmental advocacy type projects.

1316
00:46:51.520 --> 00:46:53.270
And they've always embraced solar.

1317
00:46:53.270 --> 00:46:58.270
Like Casa Pueblo has always had solar on their own facility.

1318
00:46:58.460 --> 00:47:00.650
And so after the hurricane,

1319
00:47:00.650 --> 00:47:02.460
they became sort of this energy oasis

1320
00:47:02.460 --> 00:47:06.530
in the middle of an otherwise blacked-out town.

1321
00:47:06.530 --> 00:47:08.100
And so I think that's a big part

1322
00:47:08.100 --> 00:47:10.876
of why there's such community buy-in in Adjuntas,

1323
00:47:10.876 --> 00:47:12.570
because they had months

1324
00:47:12.570 --> 00:47:14.800
where there was basically one structure in the whole city

1325
00:47:14.800 --> 00:47:16.470
that has power, and it's Casa Pueblo,

1326
00:47:16.470 --> 00:47:18.060
and it's because they embraced solar.

1327
00:47:18.060 --> 00:47:20.950
And so there's tremendous community buy-in,

1328
00:47:20.950 --> 00:47:21.970
and combined with the fact

1329
00:47:21.970 --> 00:47:23.110
that utility rates are really high,

1330
00:47:23.110 --> 00:47:24.530
people are basically paying way too much

1331
00:47:24.530 --> 00:47:25.363
for their power anyway.

1332
00:47:25.363 --> 00:47:29.090
So they're really open to other solutions.

1333
00:47:29.090 --> 00:47:32.580
And so Casa Pueblo is looking to institute a microgrid

1334
00:47:32.580 --> 00:47:33.430
in the whole city center.

1335
00:47:33.430 --> 00:47:36.890
So basically all the main businesses downtown could go

1336
00:47:36.890 --> 00:47:39.440
onto a microgrid and sort of separate

1337
00:47:39.440 --> 00:47:41.150
from the utility.

1338
00:47:41.150 --> 00:47:43.120
And the executive director

1339
00:47:43.120 --> 00:47:44.790
of the Honnold Foundation reached out to him,

1340
00:47:44.790 --> 00:47:46.540
because she heard about his work.

1341
00:47:46.540 --> 00:47:48.580
Dory.
Yeah, Dory, Dory Trimble.

1342
00:47:48.580 --> 00:47:50.752
She's incredible.

1343
00:47:50.752 --> 00:47:52.567
So she reached out to Casa Pueblo and was like,

1344
00:47:52.567 --> 00:47:54.590
"Oh, we'd love to help support this project."

1345
00:47:54.590 --> 00:47:57.200
And then we wound up wrangling Rivian,

1346
00:47:57.200 --> 00:47:58.670
which is a personal sponsor of mine,

1347
00:47:58.670 --> 00:48:00.310
the electric truck manufacturer.

1348
00:48:00.310 --> 00:48:03.390
They offered to supply the batteries for storage

1349
00:48:03.390 --> 00:48:05.430
for the project, because half the microgrid

1350
00:48:05.430 --> 00:48:07.159
is being able to store the energy they use,

1351
00:48:07.159 --> 00:48:09.727
and that's basically how it all came together.

1352
00:48:09.727 --> 00:48:11.130
Right, it's pretty cool.

1353
00:48:11.130 --> 00:48:14.950
What was really cool was hearing RJ, the founder of Rivian,

1354
00:48:14.950 --> 00:48:19.030
talk about how they've created this battery technology,

1355
00:48:19.030 --> 00:48:22.230
such that when the car has run its course

1356
00:48:22.230 --> 00:48:23.700
and people are done with it or whatever,

1357
00:48:23.700 --> 00:48:24.860
they can pull the battery out

1358
00:48:24.860 --> 00:48:28.480
and then they can repurpose it for this very thing

1359
00:48:28.480 --> 00:48:29.680
to like power these grids.

1360
00:48:29.680 --> 00:48:30.513
Yeah, that's exactly it.

1361
00:48:30.513 --> 00:48:34.290
And that's an important part of the Rivian design,

1362
00:48:34.290 --> 00:48:35.455
because some electric cars

1363
00:48:35.455 --> 00:48:37.510
haven't really designed their batteries

1364
00:48:37.510 --> 00:48:39.330
with the second life applications in mind.

1365
00:48:39.330 --> 00:48:41.350
They just make the cheapest battery they can.

1366
00:48:41.350 --> 00:48:42.400
And then when it's done,

1367
00:48:42.400 --> 00:48:44.240
it just kind of gets shredded.

1368
00:48:44.240 --> 00:48:47.170
So I think that when you go into the design process

1369
00:48:47.170 --> 00:48:49.420
with the intention of using the batteries

1370
00:48:49.420 --> 00:48:52.747
for something else useful after their life om the car,

1371
00:48:52.747 --> 00:48:54.650
that's an important, and really

1372
00:48:54.650 --> 00:48:56.970
that should be a design principle for all products.

1373
00:48:56.970 --> 00:48:58.260
Thinking about what happens to it

1374
00:48:58.260 --> 00:48:59.093
when it no longer can do

1375
00:48:59.093 --> 00:49:00.770
what it's designed for.
Especially when the mining,

1376
00:49:00.770 --> 00:49:02.150
the minerals and every, I mean.

1377
00:49:02.150 --> 00:49:03.747
it's so intensive now.
Totally, totally.

1378
00:49:03.747 --> 00:49:07.060
And the environmental impact of that is so traumatic

1379
00:49:07.060 --> 00:49:09.183
for the planet.
Totally.

1380
00:49:09.183 --> 00:49:11.070
But the thing that, I think,

1381
00:49:11.070 --> 00:49:15.000
the thing that, the biggest like personal thing

1382
00:49:15.000 --> 00:49:17.550
from the Adjuntas project,

1383
00:49:17.550 --> 00:49:18.383
I don't even know what you call it,

1384
00:49:18.383 --> 00:49:19.890
but like the thing that struck me the most

1385
00:49:19.890 --> 00:49:21.520
from the microgrid in Adjuntas

1386
00:49:21.520 --> 00:49:24.057
is that when you're in Adjuntas

1387
00:49:24.057 --> 00:49:25.140
and you're in the city center,

1388
00:49:25.140 --> 00:49:26.670
it's like a classic sort of plaza,

1389
00:49:26.670 --> 00:49:28.620
like what you think of, you know, small town America

1390
00:49:28.620 --> 00:49:30.410
or something with the city square.

1391
00:49:30.410 --> 00:49:32.580
It's like this bustling town center.

1392
00:49:32.580 --> 00:49:34.180
There are tons of cars, tons of people.

1393
00:49:34.180 --> 00:49:36.420
It's like, you know, it's the center

1394
00:49:36.420 --> 00:49:37.870
of this whole community.

1395
00:49:37.870 --> 00:49:39.680
And to power the whole thing,

1396
00:49:39.680 --> 00:49:42.390
it's something like eight Rivian truck batteries.

1397
00:49:42.390 --> 00:49:43.860
And so if you think of it as eight trucks

1398
00:49:43.860 --> 00:49:45.680
that are gonna power that whole downtown,

1399
00:49:45.680 --> 00:49:46.860
when you're standing in the downtown

1400
00:49:46.860 --> 00:49:48.140
and you look at how many cars are around you,

1401
00:49:48.140 --> 00:49:49.520
there are like hundreds of cars

1402
00:49:49.520 --> 00:49:50.802
parked on both sides of the street,

1403
00:49:50.802 --> 00:49:52.880
bumper-to-bumper going around the square.

1404
00:49:52.880 --> 00:49:54.390
It's like crazy, it's super dense, you know?

1405
00:49:54.390 --> 00:49:56.657
And you're like, "Man, eight of those

1406
00:49:56.657 --> 00:49:58.687
"can power the whole thing."

1407
00:49:58.687 --> 00:50:00.030
It's like pretty striking actually,

1408
00:50:00.030 --> 00:50:04.280
because Rivian, I think Amazon has already pre-ordered

1409
00:50:04.280 --> 00:50:07.220
100,000 electric delivery vans from Rivian,

1410
00:50:07.220 --> 00:50:08.248
maybe even more now.

1411
00:50:08.248 --> 00:50:09.200
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

1412
00:50:09.200 --> 00:50:10.180
That was a big deal.

1413
00:50:10.180 --> 00:50:12.780
That's like a huge part of Rivian's business

1414
00:50:12.780 --> 00:50:14.560
is gonna be powering the Amazon fleet.

1415
00:50:14.560 --> 00:50:17.500
Totally, but so 100,000 electric delivery vans,

1416
00:50:17.500 --> 00:50:20.412
and you think that, you know, all of those will be

1417
00:50:20.412 --> 00:50:24.217
sort of phasing out in 10, 15 years.

1418
00:50:24.217 --> 00:50:25.560
And it only takes eight of them

1419
00:50:25.560 --> 00:50:27.429
to power this whole city center

1420
00:50:27.429 --> 00:50:29.997
in a way that's like transformative for the community.

1421
00:50:29.997 --> 00:50:32.220
And you're like, when you multiply that out,

1422
00:50:32.220 --> 00:50:33.797
you're like, "Man, that's a lot of communities

1423
00:50:33.797 --> 00:50:35.657
"that could be positively impacted in that way."

1424
00:50:35.657 --> 00:50:38.360
And so that's kind of the exciting opportunity

1425
00:50:38.360 --> 00:50:39.420
for the Honnold Foundation, I think,

1426
00:50:39.420 --> 00:50:41.080
is to help establish sort of a pipeline

1427
00:50:41.080 --> 00:50:42.250
for second-life batteries

1428
00:50:42.250 --> 00:50:45.290
and potentially implement more microgrids like this

1429
00:50:45.290 --> 00:50:47.120
around say the Caribbean or something.

1430
00:50:47.120 --> 00:50:49.730
I mean, I suppose we should say,

1431
00:50:49.730 --> 00:50:52.820
you created this foundation, what like three years ago

1432
00:50:52.820 --> 00:50:53.653
or something like that?
No, no.

1433
00:50:53.653 --> 00:50:55.596
I made it like in 2012 or something.

1434
00:50:55.596 --> 00:50:56.429
Oh, it's been around that long?

1435
00:50:56.429 --> 00:50:58.667
It's been this slow-
It's kind of hit this stride

1436
00:50:58.667 --> 00:50:59.500
in the last couple years.

1437
00:50:59.500 --> 00:51:00.640
And a big part of that is hiring

1438
00:51:00.640 --> 00:51:01.740
the executive director Dory,

1439
00:51:01.740 --> 00:51:03.710
who's actually very smart and is,

1440
00:51:03.710 --> 00:51:05.560
because for many years it was just a way

1441
00:51:05.560 --> 00:51:07.520
of me personally donating money.

1442
00:51:07.520 --> 00:51:10.130
And so I would just, you know, donate some money.

1443
00:51:10.130 --> 00:51:11.700
It'd be split into let's say two grants

1444
00:51:11.700 --> 00:51:12.800
to two different organizations.

1445
00:51:12.800 --> 00:51:14.010
And like, that's it.

1446
00:51:14.010 --> 00:51:15.830
And then in the last three or four years,

1447
00:51:15.830 --> 00:51:17.060
sort of since "Free Solo"

1448
00:51:17.060 --> 00:51:20.300
and the whole crazy movie tour, it's really become

1449
00:51:20.300 --> 00:51:22.030
like much more of its own organization.

1450
00:51:22.030 --> 00:51:23.690
It started as your own personal

1451
00:51:23.690 --> 00:51:25.570
effective altruism experiment.

1452
00:51:25.570 --> 00:51:26.403
Yeah, exactly.

1453
00:51:26.403 --> 00:51:28.390
And then it's become institutionalized.

1454
00:51:28.390 --> 00:51:31.984
I think you guys donate like 1.3 million,

1455
00:51:31.984 --> 00:51:34.250
you had like 1.3 million?
Almost a million this year.

1456
00:51:34.250 --> 00:51:38.650
You donated like 87%, like a really high percentage

1457
00:51:38.650 --> 00:51:41.680
of what came in like went directly to projects.

1458
00:51:41.680 --> 00:51:43.400
And you've picked solar

1459
00:51:43.400 --> 00:51:46.274
as the primary focus at least for now.

1460
00:51:46.274 --> 00:51:49.850
So I have lots of things I want to say about that,

1461
00:51:49.850 --> 00:51:53.070
but maybe explain as somebody who's very, you know,

1462
00:51:53.070 --> 00:51:54.230
environmentally literate

1463
00:51:54.230 --> 00:51:59.230
and has spent a lot of time studying environmentalism,

1464
00:51:59.320 --> 00:52:01.130
thinking about it,

1465
00:52:01.130 --> 00:52:05.083
why is solar the primary focus right now?

1466
00:52:06.080 --> 00:52:08.653
So when I started the foundation,

1467
00:52:08.653 --> 00:52:10.260
like you said, it was just my way

1468
00:52:10.260 --> 00:52:12.970
of sort of trying to affect, you know, effective altruism.

1469
00:52:12.970 --> 00:52:14.180
So I was basically just looking

1470
00:52:14.180 --> 00:52:17.780
for any kind of, well, I guess,

1471
00:52:17.780 --> 00:52:18.613
starting at the beginning,

1472
00:52:18.613 --> 00:52:19.794
I think that the most important issue

1473
00:52:19.794 --> 00:52:21.970
facing humanity is climate change

1474
00:52:21.970 --> 00:52:23.430
or sort of environmental degradation,

1475
00:52:23.430 --> 00:52:25.050
more broadly let's say.

1476
00:52:25.050 --> 00:52:26.800
And at the same time, I think there's no point

1477
00:52:26.800 --> 00:52:29.190
in trying to solve environmental problems

1478
00:52:29.190 --> 00:52:31.100
that don't also improve quality of life

1479
00:52:31.100 --> 00:52:34.430
that like help the human populations in the area.

1480
00:52:34.430 --> 00:52:35.670
Because you know, I've been

1481
00:52:35.670 --> 00:52:37.610
on enough expeditions to various parts of the world

1482
00:52:37.610 --> 00:52:39.640
where you see communities that, you know,

1483
00:52:39.640 --> 00:52:42.500
like they'll cut down the last tree on Earth

1484
00:52:42.500 --> 00:52:44.400
if it means boiling water for their family

1485
00:52:44.400 --> 00:52:46.107
to keep their kids safe and things like that.

1486
00:52:46.107 --> 00:52:47.920
And the solution can't be premised

1487
00:52:47.920 --> 00:52:49.890
on us all being martyrs.

1488
00:52:49.890 --> 00:52:50.723
No, exactly.

1489
00:52:50.723 --> 00:52:51.556
That's the thing.

1490
00:52:51.556 --> 00:52:53.047
And anywhere you go in the world,

1491
00:52:53.047 --> 00:52:55.260
and it's funny, 'cause we're talking about Guyana.

1492
00:52:55.260 --> 00:52:56.700
Like we were just hanging with these,

1493
00:52:56.700 --> 00:52:57.730
all the Amerindian folks

1494
00:52:57.730 --> 00:52:59.100
like deep in the interior,

1495
00:52:59.100 --> 00:53:02.400
like basically Native Americans from South America.

1496
00:53:02.400 --> 00:53:04.300
And you know, a bunch of the villages,

1497
00:53:04.300 --> 00:53:06.230
they're living super traditional lifestyles.

1498
00:53:06.230 --> 00:53:08.560
It's like, you know, basically still practicing slash

1499
00:53:08.560 --> 00:53:10.820
and burn agriculture, cultivating cassava.

1500
00:53:10.820 --> 00:53:14.040
It's all, you know, as it has been for thousands of years

1501
00:53:14.040 --> 00:53:16.454
to some extent, and they're all like, "We want DirecTV."

1502
00:53:16.454 --> 00:53:17.580
And you're like, "You don't even have power."

1503
00:53:17.580 --> 00:53:20.210
Like there's no grid, there's no cell service,

1504
00:53:20.210 --> 00:53:22.120
there's no connectivity, there's no transportation.

1505
00:53:22.120 --> 00:53:23.170
You know, they have to take a boat

1506
00:53:23.170 --> 00:53:25.223
for a day to get to the closest town.

1507
00:53:26.214 --> 00:53:28.247
It's like so disconnected, and they're like,

1508
00:53:28.247 --> 00:53:30.090
"When's the DirecTV getting here?"

1509
00:53:30.090 --> 00:53:31.950
And you're like, "I think it might be a minute."

1510
00:53:31.950 --> 00:53:33.658
Like, you're like first you gotta,

1511
00:53:33.658 --> 00:53:34.890
there are a lot of things like,

1512
00:53:34.890 --> 00:53:36.266
first you need a metal roof.

1513
00:53:36.266 --> 00:53:38.817
You need to be able to keep stuff dry, you know?

1514
00:53:38.817 --> 00:53:39.919
Plumbing.
Yeah, totally.

1515
00:53:39.919 --> 00:53:41.423
General electricity.

1516
00:53:41.423 --> 00:53:44.290
So I've been on enough trips like that

1517
00:53:44.290 --> 00:53:46.630
where you realize that there are really humans

1518
00:53:46.630 --> 00:53:47.700
all over Earth, you know.

1519
00:53:47.700 --> 00:53:49.720
Even if they're actively trying

1520
00:53:49.720 --> 00:53:52.440
to preserve their traditional lifestyle,

1521
00:53:52.440 --> 00:53:54.750
like they still want refrigeration.

1522
00:53:54.750 --> 00:53:55.865
They want access to medicine.

1523
00:53:55.865 --> 00:53:56.815
They want communication.

1524
00:53:56.815 --> 00:53:59.800
Pretty much all humans want some degree

1525
00:53:59.800 --> 00:54:01.760
of material comfort in their life.

1526
00:54:01.760 --> 00:54:04.260
And that's only fair, you know.

1527
00:54:04.260 --> 00:54:06.500
It's like, I want to be relatively comfortable.

1528
00:54:06.500 --> 00:54:07.950
I want climate control,

1529
00:54:07.950 --> 00:54:09.050
at least to some extent.

1530
00:54:09.050 --> 00:54:11.102
I want like flooring.

1531
00:54:11.102 --> 00:54:12.873
I want to not get parasites, things like that.

1532
00:54:12.873 --> 00:54:15.270
Like I want access to clean food and water.

1533
00:54:15.270 --> 00:54:18.400
And so, you know, I think that when you focus

1534
00:54:18.400 --> 00:54:19.360
on environmental issues,

1535
00:54:19.360 --> 00:54:22.110
it's important to also sort of focus on the equity issues

1536
00:54:22.110 --> 00:54:24.853
of like, well, humans, like all humans should be entitled

1537
00:54:24.853 --> 00:54:27.640
to certain standards of living.

1538
00:54:27.640 --> 00:54:29.200
And so I think that if you're trying

1539
00:54:29.200 --> 00:54:30.360
to solve environmental issues

1540
00:54:30.360 --> 00:54:32.160
without also focusing on the equity side,

1541
00:54:32.160 --> 00:54:36.920
like making sure that all humans are equally able

1542
00:54:36.920 --> 00:54:38.270
to take care of themselves,

1543
00:54:39.368 --> 00:54:41.030
basically it's just not fair

1544
00:54:41.030 --> 00:54:43.900
to not do those both at the same time.

1545
00:54:43.900 --> 00:54:46.740
And so that sort of informed the initial projects

1546
00:54:46.740 --> 00:54:48.230
from the Honnold Foundation is like looking

1547
00:54:48.230 --> 00:54:49.440
for environmental projects

1548
00:54:49.440 --> 00:54:50.710
that also improve standard of living

1549
00:54:50.710 --> 00:54:52.680
that like helped folks.

1550
00:54:52.680 --> 00:54:54.090
And over the years,

1551
00:54:54.090 --> 00:54:57.150
we basically always wound up choosing solar projects,

1552
00:54:57.150 --> 00:55:00.560
because they just often are the most elegant solutions

1553
00:55:00.560 --> 00:55:02.030
to those kinds of problems

1554
00:55:02.030 --> 00:55:03.310
where it's like good for the environment,

1555
00:55:03.310 --> 00:55:06.250
good for people, you know, a clear win-win.

1556
00:55:06.250 --> 00:55:08.580
And then after several years of supporting a bunch

1557
00:55:08.580 --> 00:55:09.470
of different solar projects,

1558
00:55:09.470 --> 00:55:11.010
we were like, "We should just make this explicit."

1559
00:55:11.010 --> 00:55:13.590
'Cause at a certain point, you know, you may as well focus

1560
00:55:13.590 --> 00:55:15.260
on what you already know and what you're kind of good at.

1561
00:55:15.260 --> 00:55:19.150
And I just think solar is such an obvious solution

1562
00:55:19.150 --> 00:55:21.710
to many human problems.

1563
00:55:21.710 --> 00:55:24.390
Yeah, I mean, the sun is shining down

1564
00:55:24.390 --> 00:55:26.950
and beating us with all kinds

1565
00:55:26.950 --> 00:55:29.590
of power that we can utilize if we can figure it out.

1566
00:55:29.590 --> 00:55:33.090
And you know, what's problematic about that,

1567
00:55:33.090 --> 00:55:35.320
I mean, it seems like it's easier

1568
00:55:35.320 --> 00:55:37.020
to implement a solar solution

1569
00:55:37.020 --> 00:55:40.140
in a developing country than it is in the United States.

1570
00:55:40.140 --> 00:55:45.140
Like solar has been so problematic

1571
00:55:45.470 --> 00:55:50.096
and tricky to get implemented in houses across America.

1572
00:55:50.096 --> 00:55:51.840
Bill Maher talks about it all the time.

1573
00:55:51.840 --> 00:55:53.920
Like every show he has like a countdown.

1574
00:55:53.920 --> 00:55:54.830
He's been trying to get solar

1575
00:55:54.830 --> 00:55:56.590
at his house for like, you know, years.

1576
00:55:56.590 --> 00:55:58.870
And he's like, it's day 1000.

1577
00:55:58.870 --> 00:56:01.430
And there's all these like regulatory hurdles.

1578
00:56:01.430 --> 00:56:03.640
Like we've had a couple companies come out to our house

1579
00:56:03.640 --> 00:56:06.180
and do like an evaluation.

1580
00:56:06.180 --> 00:56:07.970
And there's always some reason why

1581
00:56:07.970 --> 00:56:10.806
it was just ridiculously expensive or way too complicated.

1582
00:56:10.806 --> 00:56:14.195
I just feel like it should be easy, you know?

1583
00:56:14.195 --> 00:56:17.900
My larger point is like why isn't it just like super easy

1584
00:56:17.900 --> 00:56:18.733
to get this done?

1585
00:56:18.733 --> 00:56:20.235
It is. I mean, I don't know.

1586
00:56:20.235 --> 00:56:21.750
I think a lot of that is personal experience

1587
00:56:21.750 --> 00:56:22.583
and depending on your home.

1588
00:56:22.583 --> 00:56:24.370
'Cause, you know, I've put solar on a couple of homes,

1589
00:56:24.370 --> 00:56:25.890
well, a bunch of homes now through the foundation

1590
00:56:25.890 --> 00:56:27.470
and then but my own as well.

1591
00:56:27.470 --> 00:56:28.567
And it's like super easy.

1592
00:56:28.567 --> 00:56:30.068
Is it?
Yeah, I mean-

1593
00:56:30.068 --> 00:56:31.740
I'd like to talk to you about who I should be calling.

1594
00:56:31.740 --> 00:56:32.573
Yeah, totally.

1595
00:56:32.573 --> 00:56:35.630
I mean, I've done most of that stuff remotely too

1596
00:56:35.630 --> 00:56:37.855
where like my home in suburban Las Vegas,

1597
00:56:37.855 --> 00:56:39.820
I was actually on a climbing trip in Wyoming

1598
00:56:39.820 --> 00:56:40.810
at the time living in the van.

1599
00:56:40.810 --> 00:56:41.830
I basically made some calls.

1600
00:56:41.830 --> 00:56:43.740
People went to the house, they installed solar,

1601
00:56:43.740 --> 00:56:45.360
turned it on, all worked fine.

1602
00:56:45.360 --> 00:56:46.900
I was like never even involved.

1603
00:56:46.900 --> 00:56:47.980
I just wired money online.

1604
00:56:47.980 --> 00:56:49.200
It was like totally chill.

1605
00:56:49.200 --> 00:56:50.281
Wow.

1606
00:56:50.281 --> 00:56:52.992
I think a lot of that just has to do with,

1607
00:56:52.992 --> 00:56:54.531
you know, where you live

1608
00:56:54.531 --> 00:56:58.770
and how difficult the system is and things like that.

1609
00:56:58.770 --> 00:57:01.310
But either way, I mean, in general, it's not that hard

1610
00:57:01.310 --> 00:57:02.143
in the U.S.

1611
00:57:02.143 --> 00:57:02.976
And I mean, and you see that,

1612
00:57:02.976 --> 00:57:05.990
because rates of solar adoption are steadily increasing.

1613
00:57:05.990 --> 00:57:07.710
It's all sort of exponential growth.

1614
00:57:07.710 --> 00:57:09.780
It's like, it seems to be doing pretty well.

1615
00:57:09.780 --> 00:57:11.920
All right, well, I need to revisit this then.

1616
00:57:11.920 --> 00:57:12.753
Yeah, you should,

1617
00:57:12.753 --> 00:57:13.810
because also the cost of panels

1618
00:57:13.810 --> 00:57:15.102
has been exponentially dropping

1619
00:57:15.102 --> 00:57:18.130
to the point that nowadays, you know,

1620
00:57:18.130 --> 00:57:20.920
the panels themselves aren't even the most expensive part

1621
00:57:20.920 --> 00:57:21.753
of the install anymore.

1622
00:57:21.753 --> 00:57:24.420
It's like the actual labor and like the racking,

1623
00:57:24.420 --> 00:57:26.000
you know, like the other things,

1624
00:57:26.000 --> 00:57:27.970
like everything else that goes into installation.

1625
00:57:27.970 --> 00:57:28.910
Right.

1626
00:57:28.910 --> 00:57:33.100
So what are the projects that you want to be working on?

1627
00:57:33.100 --> 00:57:36.890
What's the next level of what you're trying to accomplish?

1628
00:57:36.890 --> 00:57:40.450
Well, so we actually just closed our latest open call

1629
00:57:40.450 --> 00:57:43.550
for what we call the Core Fund grants,

1630
00:57:43.550 --> 00:57:44.840
which are sort of like the interesting,

1631
00:57:44.840 --> 00:57:46.320
like what we were talking about, Kara Solar,

1632
00:57:46.320 --> 00:57:50.172
the Ecuadorian Amazon solar boat project.

1633
00:57:50.172 --> 00:57:52.283
Things like that come through the Core Fund,

1634
00:57:52.283 --> 00:57:54.810
which are basically like grants around the world

1635
00:57:54.810 --> 00:57:56.290
for interesting solar projects.

1636
00:57:56.290 --> 00:57:59.017
So the latest round, you know, we'll probably fund,

1637
00:57:59.017 --> 00:58:02.000
you know, 10ish projects like that.

1638
00:58:02.000 --> 00:58:04.440
But we got, well, we got hundreds of applications,

1639
00:58:04.440 --> 00:58:05.420
and of those we probably had about 50

1640
00:58:05.420 --> 00:58:08.310
or 60 that are legitimately good applications.

1641
00:58:08.310 --> 00:58:10.410
But we can only fund the top 10 or so.

1642
00:58:10.410 --> 00:58:12.570
So really there's tons of incredible ideas

1643
00:58:12.570 --> 00:58:13.903
and good projects out there.

1644
00:58:13.903 --> 00:58:15.720
It's just a matter of having enough money

1645
00:58:15.720 --> 00:58:17.452
to actually implement them all,

1646
00:58:17.452 --> 00:58:20.619
which, you know, we've, as you said,

1647
00:58:20.619 --> 00:58:22.656
we gave away over a million dollars this year,

1648
00:58:22.656 --> 00:58:27.518
which for me personally counts as tremendous success,

1649
00:58:27.518 --> 00:58:28.970
because when I started the foundation

1650
00:58:28.970 --> 00:58:31.550
it was me donating 50K a year of just my own money.

1651
00:58:31.550 --> 00:58:34.930
So to see, you know, 20 times the impact as when I started,

1652
00:58:34.930 --> 00:58:37.047
I'm like, "Oh, that's awesome to do 20 times more work.

1653
00:58:37.047 --> 00:58:38.060
"That's great."

1654
00:58:38.060 --> 00:58:39.740
But then also knowing

1655
00:58:39.740 --> 00:58:43.173
that we could have a $10 million budget and still,

1656
00:58:44.100 --> 00:58:46.410
I think 10 probably actually would pretty much fill

1657
00:58:46.410 --> 00:58:47.670
all the things that we're seeing right now.

1658
00:58:47.670 --> 00:58:49.690
But, but you know, there are like hundreds of millions

1659
00:58:49.690 --> 00:58:51.470
of dollars worth of good projects out there

1660
00:58:51.470 --> 00:58:52.670
that that could be done.

1661
00:58:53.890 --> 00:58:57.950
There are individual changes that we can all make

1662
00:58:57.950 --> 00:58:59.270
to live more sustainably.

1663
00:58:59.270 --> 00:59:02.280
And then there are kind of institutional, social changes

1664
00:59:02.280 --> 00:59:04.430
that we need to see move forward

1665
00:59:04.430 --> 00:59:06.930
in order to really kind of affect the problem,

1666
00:59:06.930 --> 00:59:09.500
the existential crisis that we're facing.

1667
00:59:09.500 --> 00:59:13.210
And, you know, those personal changes can be

1668
00:59:13.210 --> 00:59:15.200
everything from composting to your diet

1669
00:59:15.200 --> 00:59:18.790
and where you buy your clothes and all that kind of thing.

1670
00:59:18.790 --> 00:59:21.900
One of the things that you said in some article

1671
00:59:21.900 --> 00:59:25.570
or someplace you wrote this I believe

1672
00:59:25.570 --> 00:59:27.477
that I hadn't really thought a lot about

1673
00:59:27.477 --> 00:59:30.280
but it's so obvious, which is like,

1674
00:59:30.280 --> 00:59:31.811
where are you banking?

1675
00:59:31.811 --> 00:59:34.001
Yeah, I wrote an op-ed about that.

1676
00:59:34.001 --> 00:59:35.500
Yeah, talk a little bit about that.

1677
00:59:35.500 --> 00:59:36.950
Yes, and actually I'll talk about that,

1678
00:59:36.950 --> 00:59:38.070
but then I also have some things to say

1679
00:59:38.070 --> 00:59:39.670
about personal choice like that also,

1680
00:59:39.670 --> 00:59:43.330
because so banking is pretty much the number one thing

1681
00:59:43.330 --> 00:59:45.500
that you can do for personal impact,

1682
00:59:45.500 --> 00:59:47.330
which is funny because it's so much less satisfying

1683
00:59:47.330 --> 00:59:48.650
than like changing your diet, let's say.

1684
00:59:48.650 --> 00:59:49.870
'Cause it's less obvious.

1685
00:59:49.870 --> 00:59:51.459
But the thing is wherever you bank,

1686
00:59:51.459 --> 00:59:53.910
they're using your money the whole time

1687
00:59:53.910 --> 00:59:55.080
that it sits in whatever account.

1688
00:59:55.080 --> 00:59:56.552
You put it into an account,

1689
00:59:56.552 --> 00:59:58.460
and then they're investing it in things.

1690
00:59:58.460 --> 01:00:00.400
They're spending it, they're using it basically.

1691
01:00:00.400 --> 01:00:03.630
And so, you know, a famous example is like Wells Fargo

1692
01:00:03.630 --> 01:00:05.560
with the Dakota Access Pipeline or something.

1693
01:00:05.560 --> 01:00:08.580
But in general, every bank, every major bank

1694
01:00:08.580 --> 01:00:12.300
in America is supporting both sides of the political aisle.

1695
01:00:12.300 --> 01:00:13.960
They're making political donations.

1696
01:00:13.960 --> 01:00:16.730
They're investing in fossil fuels.

1697
01:00:16.730 --> 01:00:20.120
Basically if you're using a mainstream big bank,

1698
01:00:20.120 --> 01:00:23.530
it for sure is doing stuff that you personally wouldn't do.

1699
01:00:23.530 --> 01:00:24.920
Whether you're on the left or the right,

1700
01:00:24.920 --> 01:00:26.860
it's like your bank is for sure doing stuff

1701
01:00:26.860 --> 01:00:28.420
that you wouldn't want it to do.

1702
01:00:28.420 --> 01:00:30.370
And so, you know, the solution there is

1703
01:00:30.370 --> 01:00:33.740
to do bank with nonprofit credit unions, things like that,

1704
01:00:33.740 --> 01:00:35.776
like local banks, smaller scale banks.

1705
01:00:35.776 --> 01:00:40.776
Just bank with a bank that is not going to be investing

1706
01:00:41.770 --> 01:00:44.160
in like fossil fuel infrastructure and things like that

1707
01:00:44.160 --> 01:00:46.050
that you might not personally support.

1708
01:00:46.050 --> 01:00:49.610
Right, is there a resource where you can go online

1709
01:00:49.610 --> 01:00:52.520
and get like somebody who's done like a consumer reports

1710
01:00:52.520 --> 01:00:54.480
on this where there's like a clearing house

1711
01:00:54.480 --> 01:00:56.990
of like seeing what all the banks are doing?

1712
01:00:56.990 --> 01:00:58.520
Yeah, I'm sure there is.
There must be.

1713
01:00:58.520 --> 01:00:59.720
Yeah, there must be.

1714
01:00:59.720 --> 01:01:02.170
So my sister actually many years ago

1715
01:01:02.170 --> 01:01:03.400
before I started my foundation,

1716
01:01:03.400 --> 01:01:05.140
before I started any of this stuff,

1717
01:01:05.140 --> 01:01:06.050
she gave me this book

1718
01:01:06.050 --> 01:01:07.520
called "The Better World Shopping Guide,"

1719
01:01:07.520 --> 01:01:08.750
which I think is out of print now

1720
01:01:08.750 --> 01:01:11.280
but at the time was this incredible resource

1721
01:01:11.280 --> 01:01:13.700
where for any consumer product, including banking

1722
01:01:13.700 --> 01:01:15.840
and things like that, you could flip through

1723
01:01:15.840 --> 01:01:18.480
and basically see all brands listed from A to F.

1724
01:01:18.480 --> 01:01:21.870
And it was eye-opening for me at the time,

1725
01:01:21.870 --> 01:01:23.640
because I realized I could go to the grocery store,

1726
01:01:23.640 --> 01:01:26.780
look at breads and see two different breads on the shelf,

1727
01:01:26.780 --> 01:01:30.380
same price point, same basic quality, except one, you know,

1728
01:01:30.380 --> 01:01:32.500
is basically actively supporting the type

1729
01:01:32.500 --> 01:01:33.790
of world that I want to live in.

1730
01:01:33.790 --> 01:01:37.300
You know, like paying its employees good living wages

1731
01:01:37.300 --> 01:01:39.760
and like providing maternity leave and things like that.

1732
01:01:39.760 --> 01:01:42.830
And the other is like ruthlessly exploiting its workers

1733
01:01:42.830 --> 01:01:46.160
and degrading the Earth and it's the exact same product.

1734
01:01:46.160 --> 01:01:46.993
And you're kind of like,

1735
01:01:46.993 --> 01:01:48.297
"Well, obviously I should support the one

1736
01:01:48.297 --> 01:01:50.713
"that makes for a better world."

1737
01:01:50.713 --> 01:01:53.360
And so yeah, "The Better World Shopping Guide."

1738
01:01:53.360 --> 01:01:55.120
My sister, she signed it for me.

1739
01:01:55.120 --> 01:01:58.597
It said, "For Alex, in case you ever start giving a shit.

1740
01:01:58.597 --> 01:01:59.430
"Love, Stasia."

1741
01:01:59.430 --> 01:02:01.660
Like it was a pretty classic, you know,

1742
01:02:01.660 --> 01:02:03.360
because especially when I was younger,

1743
01:02:03.360 --> 01:02:05.550
I was just all a little more like hard line.

1744
01:02:05.550 --> 01:02:06.383
I was just so focused on climbing.

1745
01:02:06.383 --> 01:02:08.960
Yeah, but you now, you're all about it.

1746
01:02:08.960 --> 01:02:10.163
Yeah, but that's the interesting thing

1747
01:02:10.163 --> 01:02:13.110
about like the long gradual awakening,

1748
01:02:13.110 --> 01:02:15.810
like you just learn more and you start to care more about it

1749
01:02:15.810 --> 01:02:18.860
and then it's sort of a virtuous cycle.

1750
01:02:18.860 --> 01:02:22.280
Yeah, well that keys into something

1751
01:02:22.280 --> 01:02:23.450
I think he wanted to talk about,

1752
01:02:23.450 --> 01:02:25.459
which is like this personal choice thing.

1753
01:02:25.459 --> 01:02:29.620
Yeah, it's funny 'cause I've sort of come full circle

1754
01:02:29.620 --> 01:02:30.453
on this a little bit,

1755
01:02:30.453 --> 01:02:32.257
because obviously I care a lot about personal choice.

1756
01:02:32.257 --> 01:02:33.590
I'm a vegetarian because

1757
01:02:33.590 --> 01:02:37.092
or like sort of aspirationally vegan basically,

1758
01:02:37.092 --> 01:02:38.720
personally because I'm lactose intolerant,

1759
01:02:38.720 --> 01:02:42.044
partially because basically just

1760
01:02:42.044 --> 01:02:45.173
it's a much lower impact for diet.

1761
01:02:46.320 --> 01:02:47.510
You know, I changed my banking.

1762
01:02:47.510 --> 01:02:49.760
I've changed all kinds of lifestyle things

1763
01:02:49.760 --> 01:02:53.100
in an effort to minimize my personal impact on Earth

1764
01:02:53.100 --> 01:02:55.220
and even starting the Honnold Foundation was to some extent

1765
01:02:55.220 --> 01:02:59.690
to know that I personally was doing as much good as harm.

1766
01:02:59.690 --> 01:03:01.820
Because just by living and traveling to climb and all that,

1767
01:03:01.820 --> 01:03:04.030
like obviously I'm having a negative impact on the world.

1768
01:03:04.030 --> 01:03:04.863
And so I'd like to think

1769
01:03:04.863 --> 01:03:07.683
that I'm doing as much good as harm.

1770
01:03:07.683 --> 01:03:10.430
But now I'm sort of coming back around to it

1771
01:03:10.430 --> 01:03:15.250
where I feel like the whole onus on personal choice

1772
01:03:15.250 --> 01:03:18.607
has been sort of foisted upon us by outside forces.

1773
01:03:18.607 --> 01:03:20.767
It's like basically industry telling us like,

1774
01:03:20.767 --> 01:03:23.590
"You should think about your choices"

1775
01:03:23.590 --> 01:03:25.290
rather than have the industry regulated

1776
01:03:25.290 --> 01:03:26.670
in a way that is appropriate.

1777
01:03:26.670 --> 01:03:28.570
You know, because even if

1778
01:03:28.570 --> 01:03:30.560
and actually COVID has been an interesting measure of this,

1779
01:03:30.560 --> 01:03:33.100
because even with travel lockdowns

1780
01:03:33.100 --> 01:03:34.986
for the whole world for the last year,

1781
01:03:34.986 --> 01:03:38.750
global emissions have only dropped seven

1782
01:03:38.750 --> 01:03:40.520
or 10% or something like 7%.

1783
01:03:40.520 --> 01:03:42.730
I mean, that's still barely in line

1784
01:03:42.730 --> 01:03:44.670
with Paris Accord type things.

1785
01:03:44.670 --> 01:03:47.270
And so if you think that all of human society

1786
01:03:47.270 --> 01:03:48.700
is fundamentally changed for the last year

1787
01:03:48.700 --> 01:03:50.209
and we're still barely hitting the numbers

1788
01:03:50.209 --> 01:03:52.150
that we pledged to meet

1789
01:03:52.150 --> 01:03:56.570
for the latest round of climate accord negotiations,

1790
01:03:56.570 --> 01:03:58.220
you're kinda like, "That's crazy."

1791
01:03:58.220 --> 01:03:59.053
You know what I mean?

1792
01:03:59.053 --> 01:04:00.260
It just shows the scale of this.

1793
01:04:00.260 --> 01:04:02.720
It shows the scale of real change that has to happen.

1794
01:04:02.720 --> 01:04:04.950
And that kind of thing only happens with policy,

1795
01:04:04.950 --> 01:04:06.560
because it's one thing for individuals

1796
01:04:06.560 --> 01:04:08.566
to like choose the right product all the time,

1797
01:04:08.566 --> 01:04:10.513
but it's another thing to just make sure

1798
01:04:10.513 --> 01:04:13.340
that all the products have to be made well to begin with.

1799
01:04:13.340 --> 01:04:14.173
You know what I mean?

1800
01:04:14.173 --> 01:04:15.320
Like eliminating pollution in different ways.

1801
01:04:15.320 --> 01:04:17.890
But policy is also a reflection

1802
01:04:17.890 --> 01:04:19.820
of popular sentiment, right?

1803
01:04:19.820 --> 01:04:22.363
Like if people are thinking harder

1804
01:04:22.363 --> 01:04:24.550
about their personal choices

1805
01:04:24.550 --> 01:04:26.140
and that becomes more important,

1806
01:04:26.140 --> 01:04:29.290
