01:13:41.057 --> 01:13:43.150
Especially when you consider that,

2066
01:13:43.150 --> 01:13:45.890
I mean, fires are a natural part of the life cycle

2067
01:13:45.890 --> 01:13:46.723
of the forest.

2068
01:13:46.723 --> 01:13:47.720
It's like when you're in the Sierra Nevada,

2069
01:13:47.720 --> 01:13:49.967
you're like, "Yeah, this is natural.

2070
01:13:49.967 --> 01:13:51.360
"It should be fine."

2071
01:13:51.360 --> 01:13:54.000
And yet, you know, the sky is blacked out

2072
01:13:54.000 --> 01:13:56.080
for almost a month in Tahoe this summer.

2073
01:13:56.080 --> 01:13:57.813
It was totally insane.

2074
01:13:57.813 --> 01:13:59.620
And you're like, "That's not natural."

2075
01:13:59.620 --> 01:14:00.610
That's different.
Totally, that's craziness.

2076
01:14:00.610 --> 01:14:01.760
A different thing.

2077
01:14:01.760 --> 01:14:03.130
Switching gears a little bit.

2078
01:14:03.130 --> 01:14:04.960
Yeah, totally from environmental apocalyptic.

2079
01:14:04.960 --> 01:14:07.100
How much more can we dig into this?

2080
01:14:07.100 --> 01:14:08.958
Dude, I could talk about it all day.

2081
01:14:08.958 --> 01:14:11.590
I could too.

2082
01:14:11.590 --> 01:14:12.580
It's like super depressing,

2083
01:14:12.580 --> 01:14:14.190
but also super important,

2084
01:14:14.190 --> 01:14:16.087
or like this is probably the most important issue facing-

2085
01:14:16.087 --> 01:14:16.950
We need to be talking about it.

2086
01:14:16.950 --> 01:14:17.910
Yeah, exactly.

2087
01:14:17.910 --> 01:14:20.830
And if that just moves the needle with one person

2088
01:14:20.830 --> 01:14:22.070
that's a win.

2089
01:14:22.070 --> 01:14:23.810
What else are we doing here?

2090
01:14:23.810 --> 01:14:25.369
What's the point of doing it?
To talk about climbing.

2091
01:14:25.369 --> 01:14:27.740
We talk about climbing.

2092
01:14:27.740 --> 01:14:29.860
You are in a unique situation though

2093
01:14:29.860 --> 01:14:32.120
because you did this crazy climb.

2094
01:14:32.120 --> 01:14:34.823
Like, you know, how do you,

2095
01:14:35.849 --> 01:14:36.910
and your life is kind of devoted

2096
01:14:36.910 --> 01:14:41.500
to iterative difficulty, like continuing to put yourself

2097
01:14:41.500 --> 01:14:45.180
in difficult situations and master those.

2098
01:14:45.180 --> 01:14:50.180
But you know, it's hard to trump free soloing El Cap.

2099
01:14:50.450 --> 01:14:52.052
So like, what are you gonna do?

2100
01:14:52.052 --> 01:14:56.270
How do you shift gears and find meaning

2101
01:14:56.270 --> 01:14:58.820
in a different way in this thing that you do?

2102
01:14:58.820 --> 01:15:00.240
I mean, you've kind of already answered it.

2103
01:15:00.240 --> 01:15:01.370
You're doing all these other things.

2104
01:15:01.370 --> 01:15:03.732
You've devoted your life to these environmental causes.

2105
01:15:03.732 --> 01:15:07.410
There's a lot in your, you live a very big life

2106
01:15:07.410 --> 01:15:09.360
that is providing meaning, not just for yourself

2107
01:15:09.360 --> 01:15:11.140
but for a lot of people.

2108
01:15:11.140 --> 01:15:15.470
But it is, you know, just from the pure athlete mindset,

2109
01:15:15.470 --> 01:15:17.000
how do you get jacked-up

2110
01:15:17.000 --> 01:15:20.660
about another adventure when you've climbed a mountain?

2111
01:15:20.660 --> 01:15:22.680
You know, you literally climbed the mountain.

2112
01:15:22.680 --> 01:15:24.880
Yeah, no, that's a totally fair question.

2113
01:15:24.880 --> 01:15:26.582
And I think it's taken, I mean,

2114
01:15:26.582 --> 01:15:28.687
I free soloed El Cap, I think, in 2017,

2115
01:15:28.687 --> 01:15:30.154
and the tour was in 2018.

2116
01:15:30.154 --> 01:15:32.542
And so it's kind of taken me a year and a half,

2117
01:15:32.542 --> 01:15:34.900
but really the season is sort of the beginning

2118
01:15:34.900 --> 01:15:37.799
of like a real fire or like real hunger

2119
01:15:37.799 --> 01:15:39.880
for challenging things again.

2120
01:15:39.880 --> 01:15:41.710
And so, I mean, you're right

2121
01:15:41.710 --> 01:15:43.960
that nothing will ever trump El Cap,

2122
01:15:43.960 --> 01:15:46.720
nothing will be better than El Cap.

2123
01:15:46.720 --> 01:15:50.270
But there's still some other things that are pretty cool

2124
01:15:50.270 --> 01:15:52.180
that I'm excited about.

2125
01:15:52.180 --> 01:15:54.080
The world's a big place.
Yeah, exactly.

2126
01:15:54.080 --> 01:15:55.800
And actually a couple of things I'm excited about right now

2127
01:15:55.800 --> 01:15:58.171
are just at home like projects in Red Rock,

2128
01:15:58.171 --> 01:16:00.670
which is the climbing area outside of Las Vegas.

2129
01:16:00.670 --> 01:16:04.970
And you know, I mean, sometimes you just get inspired

2130
01:16:04.970 --> 01:16:06.087
by certain things, and you're like,

2131
01:16:06.087 --> 01:16:07.870
"This seems crazy and really hard,"

2132
01:16:07.870 --> 01:16:09.137
but then you're like, "I think I could do it."

2133
01:16:09.137 --> 01:16:10.277
And then you're like, "Can I do it?"

2134
01:16:10.277 --> 01:16:12.031
And then you have to find out if you can do it.

2135
01:16:12.031 --> 01:16:14.780
And really that's like the whole joy

2136
01:16:14.780 --> 01:16:17.020
of the process is the finding out like, well, can I do it?

2137
01:16:17.020 --> 01:16:18.000
Is it crazy?

2138
01:16:18.000 --> 01:16:18.833
Let's find out.

2139
01:16:18.833 --> 01:16:19.666
Right.

2140
01:16:19.666 --> 01:16:21.910
Am I right in thinking that before COVID hit

2141
01:16:21.910 --> 01:16:24.550
you were thinking of doing the Seven Summits?

2142
01:16:24.550 --> 01:16:26.550
Yeah, though that, I mean,

2143
01:16:26.550 --> 01:16:27.760
I still would like to in my life.

2144
01:16:27.760 --> 01:16:32.490
That's not really like a big climbing goal, which is funny-

2145
01:16:32.490 --> 01:16:33.323
I mean, that's a whole different kind of thing.

2146
01:16:33.323 --> 01:16:36.380
Yeah, I mean, for me that stems,

2147
01:16:36.380 --> 01:16:38.067
like I don't know if you've ever read the book,

2148
01:16:38.067 --> 01:16:39.410
"The Seven Summits" by Dick Bass.

2149
01:16:39.410 --> 01:16:40.597
He was the first person-
No.

2150
01:16:40.597 --> 01:16:42.190
He's kind of a non-climber.

2151
01:16:42.190 --> 01:16:43.147
He owned Snowbird back in the day.

2152
01:16:43.147 --> 01:16:44.340
He was a businessman.

2153
01:16:44.340 --> 01:16:47.227
I think he may have been oil or something,

2154
01:16:47.227 --> 01:16:49.530
but I think he was like a Texas businessman or something.

2155
01:16:49.530 --> 01:16:51.720
But he just got this like wild idea

2156
01:16:51.720 --> 01:16:54.140
that he wanted to climb the highest points on Earth.

2157
01:16:54.140 --> 01:16:56.420
And so he was guided on them.

2158
01:16:56.420 --> 01:16:57.870
And this is like before it was the thing

2159
01:16:57.870 --> 01:16:58.703
and people did it.

2160
01:16:58.703 --> 01:16:59.536
Anyway, it's a great book.

2161
01:16:59.536 --> 01:17:00.980
It's kind of like classic adventure writing.

2162
01:17:00.980 --> 01:17:02.520
And you know, I read that many, many years ago

2163
01:17:02.520 --> 01:17:03.755
and was like, "That's awesome.

2164
01:17:03.755 --> 01:17:05.640
"I'd like to do that in my life."

2165
01:17:05.640 --> 01:17:06.640
And now it's a little bit weird,

2166
01:17:06.640 --> 01:17:08.850
because Seven Summits is so commercialized

2167
01:17:08.850 --> 01:17:11.730
that real climbers kind of thumb their nose at it.

2168
01:17:11.730 --> 01:17:12.700
Like that's not cool.

2169
01:17:12.700 --> 01:17:15.034
But I'm like, I still kind of want to, you know?

2170
01:17:15.034 --> 01:17:19.928
But that's not like a rad climbing project.

2171
01:17:19.928 --> 01:17:22.110
That's just like a personal, I'd love to do that in my life

2172
01:17:22.110 --> 01:17:23.530
but we'll see how it plays out, you know?

2173
01:17:23.530 --> 01:17:25.030
Yeah, I mean, for what you do

2174
01:17:25.030 --> 01:17:27.810
to not climb the seven tallest mountains,

2175
01:17:27.810 --> 01:17:29.170
like why wouldn't you do that?

2176
01:17:29.170 --> 01:17:32.093
Yeah, I've climbed a lot of other mountains.

2177
01:17:33.690 --> 01:17:35.360
What do you think when you see, you know,

2178
01:17:35.360 --> 01:17:38.610
remember when those images were coming through from Everest

2179
01:17:38.610 --> 01:17:39.443
and it was like the,

2180
01:17:39.443 --> 01:17:41.594
there were just so many people up there

2181
01:17:41.594 --> 01:17:43.830
and it was like a traffic jam and?

2182
01:17:43.830 --> 01:17:45.050
Yeah, that's crazy.

2183
01:17:45.050 --> 01:17:47.651
No, I find that somewhat distasteful.

2184
01:17:47.651 --> 01:17:50.580
I'm just like, oh, basically that's just not the experience

2185
01:17:50.580 --> 01:17:52.440
that I'm seeking in the mountains.

2186
01:17:52.440 --> 01:17:55.450
And especially on Everest in particular,

2187
01:17:55.450 --> 01:17:56.940
there are ropes going the whole way up.

2188
01:17:56.940 --> 01:17:59.330
So everyone's just like clipped into a rope the whole way.

2189
01:17:59.330 --> 01:18:02.020
And somebody else has put those ropes out for them.

2190
01:18:02.020 --> 01:18:04.940
And so, you know, I personally have sort of mixed feelings

2191
01:18:04.940 --> 01:18:06.670
about like outsourcing the risk

2192
01:18:06.670 --> 01:18:07.860
and the challenge of climbing.

2193
01:18:07.860 --> 01:18:09.130
Like I don't want to pay somebody else

2194
01:18:09.130 --> 01:18:10.650
to put the rope up for me.

2195
01:18:10.650 --> 01:18:13.300
It's like, if I can't put the rope up myself,

2196
01:18:13.300 --> 01:18:14.980
I probably shouldn't be climbing it.

2197
01:18:14.980 --> 01:18:16.560
And not to say that that's a standard

2198
01:18:16.560 --> 01:18:18.730
that all people should hold.

2199
01:18:18.730 --> 01:18:20.662
'Cause you know, I'm sure,

2200
01:18:20.662 --> 01:18:23.410
and I think climbing Everest is really,

2201
01:18:23.410 --> 01:18:24.580
really hard regardless.

2202
01:18:24.580 --> 01:18:25.413
You know, I think that even

2203
01:18:25.413 --> 01:18:26.950
if you are just following the rope that somebody put,

2204
01:18:26.950 --> 01:18:28.570
it's still quite difficult,

2205
01:18:28.570 --> 01:18:31.540
you know, it's a challenging physical experience.

2206
01:18:31.540 --> 01:18:32.373
But I don't know.

2207
01:18:32.373 --> 01:18:34.220
I just feel like if I want to go up there,

2208
01:18:34.220 --> 01:18:36.758
I want to like actually climb something.

2209
01:18:36.758 --> 01:18:38.290
Are you gonna climb mountains?

2210
01:18:38.290 --> 01:18:39.563
I don't think so.
Why not?

2211
01:18:39.563 --> 01:18:40.600
I don't think that's my thing, man.

2212
01:18:40.600 --> 01:18:41.893
But you could.

2213
01:18:41.893 --> 01:18:43.800
I mean I could-

2214
01:18:43.800 --> 01:18:45.023
You've got the energy for it.

2215
01:18:45.023 --> 01:18:45.981
I don't feel the pull.

2216
01:18:45.981 --> 01:18:47.170
I don't feel like the allure.

2217
01:18:47.170 --> 01:18:48.003
You don't care about getting

2218
01:18:48.003 --> 01:18:49.673
on top of things?
It doesn't like-

2219
01:18:49.673 --> 01:18:50.643
I love getting on top of things.

2220
01:18:50.643 --> 01:18:51.476
It's not my thing.

2221
01:18:51.476 --> 01:18:54.582
I want to go like in the ocean and swim down.

2222
01:18:54.582 --> 01:18:57.460
That's not my thing.
Or swim across.

2223
01:18:57.460 --> 01:19:00.190
But I don't think about going up that much.

2224
01:19:00.190 --> 01:19:01.740
It's different, you know.

2225
01:19:01.740 --> 01:19:02.890
I went climbing a couple of times

2226
01:19:02.890 --> 01:19:04.257
with some friends and I was like,

2227
01:19:04.257 --> 01:19:05.920
"I don't think this is for me."

2228
01:19:05.920 --> 01:19:07.100
That's fair.
It was weird.

2229
01:19:07.100 --> 01:19:08.903
But mountaineering though might be a,

2230
01:19:08.903 --> 01:19:11.450
'cause that's basically like ultra running,

2231
01:19:11.450 --> 01:19:13.610
like trudging up hills.
It's different.

2232
01:19:13.610 --> 01:19:17.700
I like the idea of all these like FTKs

2233
01:19:17.700 --> 01:19:18.533
that these guys, you know,

2234
01:19:18.533 --> 01:19:19.900
like Kilian's running up these mountains

2235
01:19:19.900 --> 01:19:21.250
and seeing how fast he can do it.

2236
01:19:21.250 --> 01:19:22.470
I just like the idea

2237
01:19:22.470 --> 01:19:23.830
of like creating your own adventure

2238
01:19:23.830 --> 01:19:24.790
like you were doing.

2239
01:19:24.790 --> 01:19:26.150
Like here's this thing.

2240
01:19:26.150 --> 01:19:27.210
We have all of this here.

2241
01:19:27.210 --> 01:19:29.990
Like we don't need some, you know,

2242
01:19:29.990 --> 01:19:32.270
like race or event.
Exactly.

2243
01:19:32.270 --> 01:19:33.103
Let's just figure it out.

2244
01:19:33.103 --> 01:19:34.730
So one of the things I'm playing with this season

2245
01:19:34.730 --> 01:19:37.120
is a traverse of the entire range in Red Rock,

2246
01:19:37.120 --> 01:19:40.410
which is like this actually as the crow flies,

2247
01:19:40.410 --> 01:19:42.600
it's probably only like 10 or 15 miles like linearly,

2248
01:19:42.600 --> 01:19:44.780
but you're growing up and over all these different peaks.

2249
01:19:44.780 --> 01:19:47.280
And so I haven't quite pieced it all together yet

2250
01:19:47.280 --> 01:19:48.530
'cause it's incredibly complicated,

2251
01:19:48.530 --> 01:19:50.070
route finding and climbing.

2252
01:19:50.070 --> 01:19:52.040
And I'm climbing classic climbing routes

2253
01:19:52.040 --> 01:19:53.437
and then down soloing other climbing routes

2254
01:19:53.437 --> 01:19:55.157
and going up and down to tag the different summits

2255
01:19:55.157 --> 01:19:57.310
but also do a bunch of good climbs.

2256
01:19:57.310 --> 01:19:58.930
But it's coming in

2257
01:19:58.930 --> 01:20:01.370
at like 25,000 feet of vertical or something.

2258
01:20:01.370 --> 01:20:04.810
It's like a really, really big traverse.

2259
01:20:04.810 --> 01:20:07.640
And you know, we'll see if a man should do it

2260
01:20:07.640 --> 01:20:08.877
and how exactly it shapes up.

2261
01:20:08.877 --> 01:20:10.410
But I'm like, what an adventure

2262
01:20:10.410 --> 01:20:13.500
to leave your house and then just climb this entire skyline

2263
01:20:13.500 --> 01:20:15.620
that you can see from anywhere in town.

2264
01:20:15.620 --> 01:20:17.150
It's similar to the thing you did

2265
01:20:17.150 --> 01:20:18.840
with Tommy Caldwell, right?
Exactly.

2266
01:20:18.840 --> 01:20:21.487
What is it like 17 peaks or something like that

2267
01:20:21.487 --> 01:20:24.090
and like 20K of-
Something like that.

2268
01:20:24.090 --> 01:20:25.070
Yeah, actually it was funny,

2269
01:20:25.070 --> 01:20:26.340
because with these really long

2270
01:20:26.340 --> 01:20:28.210
and technical rock climbing traverses,

2271
01:20:28.210 --> 01:20:31.700
it's actually hard to quantify the vert in the numbers,

2272
01:20:31.700 --> 01:20:33.440
because GPS doesn't really work

2273
01:20:33.440 --> 01:20:35.400
when you're climbing vertical routes.

2274
01:20:35.400 --> 01:20:37.740
You know, like when you're doing sheer vertical walls,

2275
01:20:37.740 --> 01:20:40.640
your GPS will like ping you all over the place.

2276
01:20:40.640 --> 01:20:42.080
So it like messes up all the numbers.

2277
01:20:42.080 --> 01:20:44.140
So I don't know.

2278
01:20:44.140 --> 01:20:45.350
Have you had that experience?

2279
01:20:45.350 --> 01:20:47.500
Like if you go up really steep hills with GPS-

2280
01:20:47.500 --> 01:20:49.270
I don't go up hills that steep,

2281
01:20:49.270 --> 01:20:51.310
steep enough to do that.

2282
01:20:51.310 --> 01:20:53.440
'Cause basically GPS doesn't really work

2283
01:20:53.440 --> 01:20:54.790
on a vertical plan.

2284
01:20:54.790 --> 01:20:57.563
It's like more for like a horizontal.

2285
01:20:58.447 --> 01:21:00.880
And then the thing I did with Tommy last summer

2286
01:21:00.880 --> 01:21:02.570
in Rocky Mountain National Park wound up taking us

2287
01:21:02.570 --> 01:21:03.450
like 36 hours.

2288
01:21:03.450 --> 01:21:04.830
So all of our devices died anyway.

2289
01:21:04.830 --> 01:21:06.160
'Cause your batteries don't last that long

2290
01:21:06.160 --> 01:21:07.350
if they're tracking GPS.

2291
01:21:07.350 --> 01:21:09.240
Wasn't there like a, you missed a drop-off

2292
01:21:09.240 --> 01:21:11.420
or something happened-
There was a bit of a botch.

2293
01:21:11.420 --> 01:21:13.110
And you had no lights at night and stuff?

2294
01:21:13.110 --> 01:21:15.280
Yeah, well more importantly we had no pants.

2295
01:21:15.280 --> 01:21:18.410
So we were like at 13,000 feet in running shorts all night.

2296
01:21:18.410 --> 01:21:20.363
And it was pretty character building.

2297
01:21:21.270 --> 01:21:22.910
What has Tommy been up to?

2298
01:21:22.910 --> 01:21:25.380
He's like your main, he's like your main guy, right?

2299
01:21:25.380 --> 01:21:26.920
He's like your go-to adventurer?

2300
01:21:26.920 --> 01:21:30.160
He's one of my best adventure partners for sure.

2301
01:21:30.160 --> 01:21:31.610
Actually I just climbed with him last week,

2302
01:21:31.610 --> 01:21:34.880
like the day I got home from this expedition in Guyana.

2303
01:21:34.880 --> 01:21:38.310
Tommy just happened to be in Las Vegas climbing something.

2304
01:21:38.310 --> 01:21:40.350
So we managed to sneak out for a day.

2305
01:21:40.350 --> 01:21:43.420
But he's been doing some home repair.

2306
01:21:43.420 --> 01:21:44.327
He's like doing something,

2307
01:21:44.327 --> 01:21:46.680
but he's doing a plumbing project on his house.

2308
01:21:46.680 --> 01:21:48.490
Hasn't been climbing that much this winter.

2309
01:21:48.490 --> 01:21:51.204
But he's just such an incredible climber,

2310
01:21:51.204 --> 01:21:54.208
even relatively off the couch, he's just like,

2311
01:21:54.208 --> 01:21:56.650
he's done it his whole life.

2312
01:21:56.650 --> 01:21:59.565
And are you doing, back to the environmental stuff,

2313
01:21:59.565 --> 01:22:03.460
are you doing another podcast with the Washington Post?

2314
01:22:03.460 --> 01:22:04.930
With that, I'm not sure.

2315
01:22:04.930 --> 01:22:06.110
There's nothing official.

2316
01:22:06.110 --> 01:22:08.500
I might be and I might be yeah,

2317
01:22:08.500 --> 01:22:10.710
basically interviewing environmental leaders

2318
01:22:10.710 --> 01:22:13.210
or sort of scientists about climate change, I think.

2319
01:22:13.210 --> 01:22:16.370
Which if it happens, I'd be pretty excited about

2320
01:22:16.370 --> 01:22:17.600
but honestly sort of intimidated.

2321
01:22:17.600 --> 01:22:19.250
Because as you know,

2322
01:22:19.250 --> 01:22:21.610
most podcasting is just chit-chatting with people.

2323
01:22:21.610 --> 01:22:25.530
You rarely have to know a subject super well.

2324
01:22:25.530 --> 01:22:28.054
But the thing is you don't have to hold yourself out.

2325
01:22:28.054 --> 01:22:30.750
They'll be so excited to talk to you.

2326
01:22:30.750 --> 01:22:32.530
And they know that you're not, you know,

2327
01:22:32.530 --> 01:22:34.720
a PhD in whatever it is that they are.

2328
01:22:34.720 --> 01:22:36.550
And so they'll explain it to you.

2329
01:22:36.550 --> 01:22:37.383
Yeah, but you think you'd feel like-

2330
01:22:37.383 --> 01:22:38.360
And you're trying to be a cipher

2331
01:22:38.360 --> 01:22:39.860
for the audience anyway.
That's true.

2332
01:22:39.860 --> 01:22:40.714
But you still feel

2333
01:22:40.714 --> 01:22:42.040
like you should have enough of an understanding

2334
01:22:42.040 --> 01:22:43.703
to at least ask interesting questions.

2335
01:22:43.703 --> 01:22:44.540
Yeah, but you read books.

2336
01:22:44.540 --> 01:22:45.373
You'll be fine.

2337
01:22:46.304 --> 01:22:49.040
We'll see if that, I don't know.

2338
01:22:49.040 --> 01:22:51.210
Speaking of that, like on this deep dive

2339
01:22:51.210 --> 01:22:52.300
that you did this past year,

2340
01:22:52.300 --> 01:22:53.490
what have you learned

2341
01:22:53.490 --> 01:22:55.960
that you didn't know like before COVID

2342
01:22:55.960 --> 01:22:58.040
when it comes to the environmental stuff?

2343
01:22:58.040 --> 01:22:58.873
I don't know.

2344
01:22:59.810 --> 01:23:00.643
That's an interesting question.

2345
01:23:00.643 --> 01:23:01.990
I mean, so on this trip in Guyana,

2346
01:23:01.990 --> 01:23:03.910
I just finished this book "Energy and Civilization,"

2347
01:23:03.910 --> 01:23:06.280
this like Vaclav Smil's, like this super dense tome

2348
01:23:06.280 --> 01:23:08.240
about sort of the progression

2349
01:23:08.240 --> 01:23:11.911
of different energy systems in human civilization.

2350
01:23:11.911 --> 01:23:14.130
And it was interesting.

2351
01:23:14.130 --> 01:23:17.120
I mean, I think that book gave me an appreciation

2352
01:23:17.120 --> 01:23:20.570
of how long and slowly the transitions

2353
01:23:20.570 --> 01:23:24.490
between energy systems are,

2354
01:23:24.490 --> 01:23:27.290
basically like going from human power

2355
01:23:27.290 --> 01:23:29.510
to like the introduction of the steam engine

2356
01:23:29.510 --> 01:23:33.535
to coal fired to eventually, you know, oil,

2357
01:23:33.535 --> 01:23:35.604
like full-on fossil fuels.

2358
01:23:35.604 --> 01:23:38.631
It's just like each transition takes quite a long time.

2359
01:23:38.631 --> 01:23:41.770
And it's given me an interesting perspective, I guess,

2360
01:23:41.770 --> 01:23:43.920
on like our current transition to renewables

2361
01:23:43.920 --> 01:23:46.410
and what that will take.

2362
01:23:46.410 --> 01:23:47.790
And one of the interesting things I got

2363
01:23:47.790 --> 01:23:49.470
from the book was that, you know,

2364
01:23:49.470 --> 01:23:51.890
every transition has been powered

2365
01:23:51.890 --> 01:23:55.610
by the previous fuel, let's say.

2366
01:23:55.610 --> 01:23:58.274
So like the transition to coal use was like,

2367
01:23:58.274 --> 01:24:00.926
you know, powered by steam engines

2368
01:24:00.926 --> 01:24:02.610
and whatever else.

2369
01:24:02.610 --> 01:24:05.316
You're reliant on the-
The previous energy model,

2370
01:24:05.316 --> 01:24:08.170
which is funny because that's a common criticism

2371
01:24:08.170 --> 01:24:09.007
of renewable is it's like,

2372
01:24:09.007 --> 01:24:11.820
"Oh, well it's all based on fossil fuels."

2373
01:24:11.820 --> 01:24:14.107
And you're kinda like, "Well, yeah, because any transition

2374
01:24:14.107 --> 01:24:17.520
"is gonna be based on the previous system,"

2375
01:24:17.520 --> 01:24:19.410
which I found slightly heartening in a way,

2376
01:24:19.410 --> 01:24:21.003
because I've always found that like, you know,

2377
01:24:21.003 --> 01:24:22.520
it's a little bit of a bummer that

2378
01:24:22.520 --> 01:24:26.293
to build wind turbines, let's say,

2379
01:24:26.293 --> 01:24:29.770
you have to mill a bunch of steel.

2380
01:24:29.770 --> 01:24:32.700
And like that steel is all being powered by,

2381
01:24:32.700 --> 01:24:34.470
those plants are all being powered by fossil fuels.

2382
01:24:34.470 --> 01:24:35.303
And so you're kinda like,

2383
01:24:35.303 --> 01:24:36.657
"Is it even worth building wind turbines

2384
01:24:36.657 --> 01:24:39.767
"if all the raw materials behind it

2385
01:24:39.767 --> 01:24:43.460
"are being mined and milled through fossil fuel extraction?"

2386
01:24:43.460 --> 01:24:44.630
You're like, "Is that worth it?"

2387
01:24:44.630 --> 01:24:45.780
You're kind of like, yeah.

2388
01:24:45.780 --> 01:24:46.760
I mean basically-

2389
01:24:46.760 --> 01:24:48.100
It's like a necessary evil

2390
01:24:48.100 --> 01:24:49.500
of iterating to the next thing.

2391
01:24:49.500 --> 01:24:53.160
I mean, that's a big thing with the electric cars.

2392
01:24:53.160 --> 01:24:54.930
Who's powering the Tesla grid?

2393
01:24:54.930 --> 01:24:56.530
What's the carbon footprint of that?

2394
01:24:56.530 --> 01:24:58.210
Like, it's massive.

2395
01:24:58.210 --> 01:24:59.060
Though that though, I mean,

2396
01:24:59.060 --> 01:25:00.310
when you do the math on it,

2397
01:25:00.310 --> 01:25:01.570
even if it's all powered by coal,

2398
01:25:01.570 --> 01:25:05.420
it's still better than an internal combustion engine car.

2399
01:25:05.420 --> 01:25:07.650
So, you know, it's like, yeah,

2400
01:25:07.650 --> 01:25:09.236
it's still a step in the right direction.

2401
01:25:09.236 --> 01:25:12.400
But I think the takeaway with all those, you know,

2402
01:25:12.400 --> 01:25:14.360
sort of the messy technical questions

2403
01:25:14.360 --> 01:25:16.210
of energy transition is like,

2404
01:25:16.210 --> 01:25:18.100
you still have to take these small steps

2405
01:25:18.100 --> 01:25:21.039
in a different direction if you're ever gonna get anywhere.

2406
01:25:21.039 --> 01:25:24.140
It's like, if you're hoping to decarbonize a grid

2407
01:25:24.140 --> 01:25:26.250
or change energy systems,

2408
01:25:26.250 --> 01:25:28.428
it's like, you have to start taking some steps,

2409
01:25:28.428 --> 01:25:29.720
even if they're not perfect,

2410
01:25:29.720 --> 01:25:32.430
even if it's not 100% correct.

2411
01:25:32.430 --> 01:25:34.670
You have to at least move in a direction.

2412
01:25:34.670 --> 01:25:37.290
And an appreciation that these things take time.

2413
01:25:37.290 --> 01:25:38.480
Yeah. No, totally.

2414
01:25:38.480 --> 01:25:39.970
I mean, it's not that removed

2415
01:25:39.970 --> 01:25:41.360
from like athletic performance, you know?

2416
01:25:41.360 --> 01:25:42.697
It's like, if you want to get better at something,

2417
01:25:42.697 --> 01:25:44.570
you have to just put in the days

2418
01:25:44.570 --> 01:25:46.160
like grinding away, you know, training.

2419
01:25:46.160 --> 01:25:47.970
It's like, you know, some days you suck,

2420
01:25:47.970 --> 01:25:50.600
and you just keep grinding away at it and eventually,

2421
01:25:50.600 --> 01:25:52.027
you know, hopefully eventually you do something

2422
01:25:52.027 --> 01:25:53.730
that's actually meaningful.

2423
01:25:53.730 --> 01:25:56.010
Have you gotten your Rivian truck yet?

2424
01:25:56.010 --> 01:25:56.843
No. No.

2425
01:25:56.843 --> 01:25:58.801
When is he gonna start shipping those things?

2426
01:25:58.801 --> 01:26:00.713
In June, I think.

2427
01:26:00.713 --> 01:26:02.517
I just got an email that was like,

2428
01:26:02.517 --> 01:26:04.580
"Configure your pre-order blah, blah blah."

2429
01:26:04.580 --> 01:26:06.120
I figured it's got to be getting close,

2430
01:26:06.120 --> 01:26:07.870
because RJ started posting a lot,

2431
01:26:07.870 --> 01:26:09.810
like taking the trucks out and like yeah.

2432
01:26:09.810 --> 01:26:11.820
And I was like, "Oh, they must be getting ready."

2433
01:26:11.820 --> 01:26:13.987
'Cause like it's been underground for a long time.

2434
01:26:13.987 --> 01:26:15.580
No, it's happening.

2435
01:26:15.580 --> 01:26:17.440
I just configured my pre-order.

2436
01:26:17.440 --> 01:26:18.380
Oh, you did?
Yeah.

2437
01:26:18.380 --> 01:26:19.680
It's pretty cool what they've done.

2438
01:26:19.680 --> 01:26:20.890
So they took over,

2439
01:26:20.890 --> 01:26:23.690
like they took over like an old, I don't know,

2440
01:26:23.690 --> 01:26:25.340
Nissan factory or something like that,

2441
01:26:25.340 --> 01:26:26.480
like outside of Chicago

2442
01:26:26.480 --> 01:26:28.800
and like just retrofitted the whole thing

2443
01:26:28.800 --> 01:26:30.210
and made it like sustainable

2444
01:26:30.210 --> 01:26:32.390
and brought in like amazing chefs

2445
01:26:32.390 --> 01:26:35.160
and like used materials from the area

2446
01:26:35.160 --> 01:26:38.400
to recyclable materials

2447
01:26:38.400 --> 01:26:41.550
and wood that was in the vicinity

2448
01:26:41.550 --> 01:26:43.500
to build the whole place out.

2449
01:26:43.500 --> 01:26:44.333
Totally.

2450
01:26:44.333 --> 01:26:45.620
Have you been to the factory?

2451
01:26:45.620 --> 01:26:47.180
I haven't been to the manufacturing plant.

2452
01:26:47.180 --> 01:26:49.830
I've been to the headquarters, which is outside of Detroit,

2453
01:26:49.830 --> 01:26:52.817
sort of classic auto manufacturer, you know.

2454
01:26:52.817 --> 01:26:54.647
No, it's super,

2455
01:26:54.647 --> 01:26:56.856
I mean, when I went to the headquarters

2456
01:26:56.856 --> 01:26:59.165
for the company outside of Detroit,

2457
01:26:59.165 --> 01:27:02.220
it was like full-on vision of futurist.

2458
01:27:02.220 --> 01:27:04.980
It's like kind of what you would expect from like a new,

2459
01:27:04.980 --> 01:27:07.280
you know, technological startup started happening.

2460
01:27:07.280 --> 01:27:08.410
I was like blown away.

2461
01:27:08.410 --> 01:27:09.820
I had this VR experience there

2462
01:27:09.820 --> 01:27:11.030
where I was like in a room,

2463
01:27:11.030 --> 01:27:12.350
and I put on like a VR headset

2464
01:27:12.350 --> 01:27:14.770
and then basically like sampled an assortment

2465
01:27:14.770 --> 01:27:19.500
of their like futuristic ideas, like concept vehicles.

2466
01:27:19.500 --> 01:27:22.290
But you could like walk around the space with a VR headset

2467
01:27:22.290 --> 01:27:24.640
and like interact with their concept vehicles.

2468
01:27:24.640 --> 01:27:26.250
And I was like, totally blew my mind.

2469
01:27:26.250 --> 01:27:28.305
I was like, "Is this Star Trek?"

2470
01:27:28.305 --> 01:27:29.930
It's like, "Where am I?"

2471
01:27:29.930 --> 01:27:33.190
In a classic, like nice new facility with,

2472
01:27:33.190 --> 01:27:37.100
it's like just clean and well-lit and classy

2473
01:27:37.100 --> 01:27:38.610
with like electric trucks all over.

2474
01:27:38.610 --> 01:27:40.100
And I was like, this is so-

2475
01:27:40.100 --> 01:27:42.619
Right, like not what you think of Detroit-

2476
01:27:42.619 --> 01:27:44.800
Yeah, so futuristic.
And the auto industry.

2477
01:27:44.800 --> 01:27:46.560
No, it was like, this is the future for sure.

2478
01:27:46.560 --> 01:27:48.240
That's cool.
It's really cool.

2479
01:27:48.240 --> 01:27:51.470
Have you done any VR stuff around climbing?

2480
01:27:51.470 --> 01:27:53.782
Actually I'm supposed to be doing a VR project this year,

2481
01:27:53.782 --> 01:27:54.747
like filming in the next month

2482
01:27:54.747 --> 01:27:56.690
and then something in Europe.

2483
01:27:56.690 --> 01:27:57.680
That's cool.

2484
01:27:57.680 --> 01:27:59.710
Yeah, I think it actually has the potential

2485
01:27:59.710 --> 01:28:01.340
to be really cool, but we'll see.

2486
01:28:01.340 --> 01:28:04.640
Yeah, I had this guy Michael Muller in here,

2487
01:28:04.640 --> 01:28:06.290
who's a big photographer.

2488
01:28:06.290 --> 01:28:07.123
With the shark stuff?

2489
01:28:07.123 --> 01:28:08.760
Yeah, so he showed me his stuff.

2490
01:28:08.760 --> 01:28:11.260
Dude, it's VR shark tank.
It's insane.

2491
01:28:11.260 --> 01:28:12.240
Yeah, totally blows your mind.

2492
01:28:12.240 --> 01:28:14.470
Yeah, he actually, he showed me the same thing.

2493
01:28:14.470 --> 01:28:15.303
You put it on,

2494
01:28:15.303 --> 01:28:17.570
you're like, "Oh," it's like pretty mega.

2495
01:28:17.570 --> 01:28:18.403
Right.

2496
01:28:18.403 --> 01:28:21.600
And they're using it for like PTSD in people,

2497
01:28:21.600 --> 01:28:23.217
not just like an experience-
To trigger PTSD?

2498
01:28:23.217 --> 01:28:26.018
No, to like help with people's fear responses.

2499
01:28:26.018 --> 01:28:27.810
It's pretty interesting.

2500
01:28:27.810 --> 01:28:30.157
And so I was just imagining like that

2501
01:28:30.157 --> 01:28:31.600
in the climbing context.

2502
01:28:31.600 --> 01:28:32.700
Somebody who's afraid of heights

2503
01:28:32.700 --> 01:28:34.080
or like that's their big thing-

2504
01:28:34.080 --> 01:28:35.180
I think if somebody's afraid of heights,

2505
01:28:35.180 --> 01:28:37.314
they probably should not watch my VR experience.

2506
01:28:37.314 --> 01:28:39.262
I think that might be a bit much.

2507
01:28:39.262 --> 01:28:40.823
(laughs) I know.

2508
01:28:40.823 --> 01:28:42.970
But you know, listen,

2509
01:28:42.970 --> 01:28:44.990
to have that, like somebody who's not able

2510
01:28:44.990 --> 01:28:46.040
to ever have that kind of,

2511
01:28:46.040 --> 01:28:47.440
somebody who's in a wheelchair or whatever,

2512
01:28:47.440 --> 01:28:49.090
never gonna be able to have that experience

2513
01:28:49.090 --> 01:28:51.261
to be able to like feel-
Totally.

2514
01:28:51.261 --> 01:28:52.640
What that feels like.

2515
01:28:52.640 --> 01:28:53.880
Or even just a gym climber

2516
01:28:53.880 --> 01:28:55.650
who's just realistically never gonna go

2517
01:28:55.650 --> 01:28:56.630
to some of these places.

2518
01:28:56.630 --> 01:29:01.544
Like the guy that I'm working with on the VR experience

2519
01:29:01.544 --> 01:29:03.680
shot an Everest VR piece,

2520
01:29:03.680 --> 01:29:05.880
like a three episode, you know,

2521
01:29:05.880 --> 01:29:08.271
basically he goes to the summit of Mount Everest in VR.

2522
01:29:08.271 --> 01:29:11.630
And I found it incredibly immersive and rich

2523
01:29:11.630 --> 01:29:13.180
in a way that I did not expect.

2524
01:29:13.180 --> 01:29:15.560
Because I've read tons of books about Everest

2525
01:29:15.560 --> 01:29:19.550
over the years and then to actually be in it in VR

2526
01:29:19.550 --> 01:29:20.500
and to be able to look around

2527
01:29:20.500 --> 01:29:22.120
and like interact with the landscape.

2528
01:29:22.120 --> 01:29:23.327
I was like, "This is so much better

2529
01:29:23.327 --> 01:29:24.385
"than all the books I've read."

2530
01:29:24.385 --> 01:29:25.790
I was like, "This is crazy."

2531
01:29:25.790 --> 01:29:26.623
That's the future.

2532
01:29:26.623 --> 01:29:28.480
I mean, those experiences are gonna be there for us

2533
01:29:28.480 --> 01:29:30.350
in the next five or 10 years.

2534
01:29:30.350 --> 01:29:32.000
Yeah, I mean like wearing noise-canceling headphones

2535
01:29:32.000 --> 01:29:34.500
with a good headset with like hi-res, really good,

2536
01:29:35.390 --> 01:29:36.830
you know, not too like jerky.

2537
01:29:36.830 --> 01:29:39.260
And I mean like obviously the technical side

2538
01:29:39.260 --> 01:29:40.940
of it will only improve.

2539
01:29:40.940 --> 01:29:41.773
And you're just like,

2540
01:29:41.773 --> 01:29:43.653
"It's just so real."
It's getting there.

2541
01:29:43.653 --> 01:29:45.110
It's like, it's still not quite there,

2542
01:29:45.110 --> 01:29:46.970
but I feel like they're right on the precipice

2543
01:29:46.970 --> 01:29:48.400
of it just being-

2544
01:29:48.400 --> 01:29:50.160
Yeah, I've always felt like it wasn't quite there

2545
01:29:50.160 --> 01:29:51.685
and then I watched the VR experience,

2546
01:29:51.685 --> 01:29:53.480
and I was like, "This is pretty there."

2547
01:29:53.480 --> 01:29:54.720
And that's kind of what inspired me

2548
01:29:54.720 --> 01:29:58.604
to feel like it was worth shooting a VR piece.

2549
01:29:58.604 --> 01:29:59.437
So it was like,

2550
01:29:59.437 --> 01:30:01.762
even if the current headsets aren't perfect,

2551
01:30:01.762 --> 01:30:03.690
someone will be able to use that footage

2552
01:30:03.690 --> 01:30:05.743
on better models soon.

2553
01:30:06.734 --> 01:30:08.440
It's pretty incredible.

2554
01:30:08.440 --> 01:30:09.692
Yeah, that's cool.

2555
01:30:09.692 --> 01:30:10.525
That's cool.

2556
01:30:10.525 --> 01:30:11.358
So when you do that then like,

2557
01:30:11.358 --> 01:30:13.150
so if you were gonna produce that,

2558
01:30:13.150 --> 01:30:15.100
you got to get up on some wall

2559
01:30:15.100 --> 01:30:17.230
and then you have like some kind

2560
01:30:17.230 --> 01:30:19.030
of headset with cameras all around?

2561
01:30:19.030 --> 01:30:19.988
How do they do that?

2562
01:30:19.988 --> 01:30:21.370
No, it's the opposite.

2563
01:30:21.370 --> 01:30:22.920
So the idea is that the subject

2564
01:30:22.920 --> 01:30:24.150
like climbs through the frame.

2565
01:30:24.150 --> 01:30:25.930
So the camera's fixed in a certain place,

2566
01:30:25.930 --> 01:30:28.890
and it's like full 360 camera, crazy VR setup.

2567
01:30:28.890 --> 01:30:32.150
And then the climber climbs through the frame.

2568
01:30:32.150 --> 01:30:32.983
I got it.

2569
01:30:32.983 --> 01:30:35.570
And that's to apparently to minimize motion sickness,

2570
01:30:35.570 --> 01:30:39.350
because if the subject itself is the VR focus,

2571
01:30:39.350 --> 01:30:42.010
like basically if you're wearing the VR headset

2572
01:30:42.010 --> 01:30:43.360
and everything's moving around you,

2573
01:30:43.360 --> 01:30:44.860
you get incredibly motion sick.

2574
01:30:44.860 --> 01:30:46.380
But if, when you're wearing the headset,

2575
01:30:46.380 --> 01:30:47.677
you feel like you're in a stable place

2576
01:30:47.677 --> 01:30:50.490
and you're able to control the vision and like look around

2577
01:30:50.490 --> 01:30:52.750
and then you can just see someone doing an action

2578
01:30:52.750 --> 01:30:54.760
in your frame, then it makes you way less sick.

2579
01:30:54.760 --> 01:30:55.960
I see.

2580
01:30:55.960 --> 01:30:58.030
But how would they do that on a big wall?

2581
01:30:58.030 --> 01:30:59.100
The lights.

2582
01:30:59.100 --> 01:30:59.950
It's hard work.

2583
01:30:59.950 --> 01:31:01.145
Yeah, I would imagine.

2584
01:31:01.145 --> 01:31:03.610
You have to like rig a separate repel line

2585
01:31:03.610 --> 01:31:06.020
near the route, fix the camera to the wall

2586
01:31:06.020 --> 01:31:07.480
like near the climbing route

2587
01:31:07.480 --> 01:31:08.720
and then sort of center the camera

2588
01:31:08.720 --> 01:31:09.920
so that you're looking at the climber

2589
01:31:09.920 --> 01:31:11.377
as the climber goes up the wall.

2590
01:31:11.377 --> 01:31:12.950
The camera remains static

2591
01:31:12.950 --> 01:31:13.883
or can it move too?
Yeah,

2592
01:31:13.883 --> 01:31:14.716
the camera remains static.

2593
01:31:14.716 --> 01:31:17.010
It seems like they could do some stuff with drones too.

2594
01:31:17.010 --> 01:31:18.454
No, no, it's too shaky.

2595
01:31:18.454 --> 01:31:21.970
And the point is for the camera to stay totally static.

2596
01:31:21.970 --> 01:31:23.964
Because ideally I think for the viewer,

2597
01:31:23.964 --> 01:31:26.310
they want to be in like a swivel chair or something

2598
01:31:26.310 --> 01:31:29.180
or like standing in a room where they can fully move around

2599
01:31:29.180 --> 01:31:31.630
at their own pace and the way they want.

2600
01:31:31.630 --> 01:31:33.440
And there'll be something happening in the frame

2601
01:31:33.440 --> 01:31:35.040
that's like the movie.

2602
01:31:35.040 --> 01:31:36.270
But they can also just not watch the movie

2603
01:31:36.270 --> 01:31:37.670
and just look behind them

2604
01:31:37.670 --> 01:31:38.660
and like look at the view the whole time.

2605
01:31:38.660 --> 01:31:39.493
Right, right, right.

2606
01:31:39.493 --> 01:31:40.863
But I think with the Muller stuff,

2607
01:31:40.863 --> 01:31:43.020
it's his point of view,

2608
01:31:43.020 --> 01:31:45.000
like someone outside of the cages and all that.

2609
01:31:45.000 --> 01:31:46.549
Yeah, yeah. Different deal.

2610
01:31:46.549 --> 01:31:49.140
But maybe that's also why his felt so traumatic to me.

2611
01:31:49.140 --> 01:31:51.610
It's because it like makes you vaguely motion sick.

2612
01:31:51.610 --> 01:31:52.941
And like, it's all kind of crazy.

2613
01:31:52.941 --> 01:31:54.907
The trauma might have something to do with the fact

2614
01:31:54.907 --> 01:31:59.200
that he's outside of a cage and he's with great whites.

2615
01:31:59.200 --> 01:32:01.619
Yeah, that too.
It's unbelievable.

2616
01:32:01.619 --> 01:32:03.970
No, I think the climbing stuff

2617
01:32:03.970 --> 01:32:04.803
that we'll be shooting is sort

2618
01:32:04.803 --> 01:32:05.710
of the other end of the spectrum

2619
01:32:05.710 --> 01:32:07.940
where it's like more expansive, broader views.

2620
01:32:07.940 --> 01:32:09.700
You know, a lot of it is about putting somebody

2621
01:32:09.700 --> 01:32:12.770
in a position that they could never see.

2622
01:32:12.770 --> 01:32:15.360
Like the Muller shark stuff feels very

2623
01:32:15.360 --> 01:32:17.911
like intimate and tight.

2624
01:32:17.911 --> 01:32:19.267
You're like, "Oh my God, the shark's about to eat me."

2625
01:32:19.267 --> 01:32:21.850
It's like super close and seems scary.

2626
01:32:21.850 --> 01:32:23.790
I think the climbing footage is more about being

2627
01:32:23.790 --> 01:32:25.060
in a spectacular place

2628
01:32:25.060 --> 01:32:26.930
and having super broad vistas-
I got it.

2629
01:32:26.930 --> 01:32:28.800
Yeah, where you can like see the world around you

2630
01:32:28.800 --> 01:32:29.633
and then also witness

2631
01:32:29.633 --> 01:32:31.883
somebody climbing something totally spectacular.

2632
01:32:33.800 --> 01:32:35.784
Unless we get a great white to make a cameo.

2633
01:32:35.784 --> 01:32:37.170
(Rich laughs)

2634
01:32:37.170 --> 01:32:38.003
We'll just see.

2635
01:32:38.003 --> 01:32:38.980
Combine these two worlds.

2636
01:32:38.980 --> 01:32:40.490
Yeah, exactly.
Two in a day.

2637
01:32:40.490 --> 01:32:41.604
Shark climbers.

2638
01:32:41.604 --> 01:32:44.820
You're in a, speaking of production, you're in LA.

2639
01:32:44.820 --> 01:32:46.970
You're doing like "Leno's Garage"?

2640
01:32:46.970 --> 01:32:48.026
I guess.

2641
01:32:48.026 --> 01:32:48.990
What is that about?

2642
01:32:48.990 --> 01:32:50.000
I honestly don't know.

2643
01:32:50.000 --> 01:32:51.416
I just do what I'm told.

2644
01:32:51.416 --> 01:32:53.375
They're like, "We want you to be on the show."

2645
01:32:53.375 --> 01:32:54.208
So you're gonna go

2646
01:32:54.208 --> 01:32:55.990
to like his warehouse and like see all his cars?

2647
01:32:55.990 --> 01:32:57.720
Yeah, I think "Jay Leno's Garage" is where

2648
01:32:57.720 --> 01:32:59.010
he like chats with people about the cars.

2649
01:32:59.010 --> 01:33:00.990
And I think he's a car collector.

2650
01:33:00.990 --> 01:33:02.230
So he has all kinds of interesting-

2651
01:33:02.230 --> 01:33:03.800
He's kind of a car collector.

2652
01:33:03.800 --> 01:33:06.210
He's got like multiple warehouses

2653
01:33:06.210 --> 01:33:08.740
or like hundreds of cars, like crazy.

2654
01:33:08.740 --> 01:33:10.850
He has one of the craziest car collections

2655
01:33:10.850 --> 01:33:11.810
in the world, yeah.
Really?

2656
01:33:11.810 --> 01:33:13.460
Well, so I know nothing about cars,

2657
01:33:13.460 --> 01:33:15.275
and I have my scrappy van.

2658
01:33:15.275 --> 01:33:16.108
(laughs) I know.

2659
01:33:16.108 --> 01:33:19.796
That's why I find this so interesting and hilarious.

2660
01:33:19.796 --> 01:33:20.629
But I think there's something to be said

2661
01:33:20.629 --> 01:33:23.307
for talking about, you know, the utility of cars.

2662
01:33:23.307 --> 01:33:25.570
'Cause basically I don't care at all about my car,

2663
01:33:25.570 --> 01:33:27.190
but I care about what it allows me to do

2664
01:33:27.190 --> 01:33:28.704
and the life that it allows me to lead,

2665
01:33:28.704 --> 01:33:31.280
like being on the road and be able to climb full time.

2666
01:33:31.280 --> 01:33:32.360
And I'm sure that

2667
01:33:32.360 --> 01:33:35.310
or let's hope that that's a useful perspective for his show.

2668
01:33:35.310 --> 01:33:36.143
You know, where I'm like,

2669
01:33:36.143 --> 01:33:37.027
"Oh, it's not so much about the car.

2670
01:33:37.027 --> 01:33:38.704
"It's about what I get to do with the car."

2671
01:33:38.704 --> 01:33:41.380
He's got all these crazy antique cars

2672
01:33:41.380 --> 01:33:44.150
like steam-powered engines and all this wild stuff.

2673
01:33:44.150 --> 01:33:48.920
And he, like every weekend, there's this spot

2674
01:33:48.920 --> 01:33:50.410
that's not far from where I live,

2675
01:33:50.410 --> 01:33:52.440
where a lot of like motorcyclists

2676
01:33:52.440 --> 01:33:53.980
and car enthusiasts go.

2677
01:33:53.980 --> 01:33:55.350
It's called the Rock Store.

2678
01:33:55.350 --> 01:33:56.887
It's like a little bar cafe or whatever.

2679
01:33:56.887 --> 01:33:58.770
And it's like right where all these windy, cool,

2680
01:33:58.770 --> 01:34:00.200
like canyon roads are.

2681
01:34:00.200 --> 01:34:03.388
So he like drives past my house like every weekend.

2682
01:34:03.388 --> 01:34:05.050
And if you're just at the right place

2683
01:34:05.050 --> 01:34:06.160
at the right time, you'll see him.

2684
01:34:06.160 --> 01:34:06.993
But it's always like

2685
01:34:06.993 --> 01:34:09.639
some completely crazy car-
Crazy car.

2686
01:34:09.639 --> 01:34:11.780
That shouldn't, I'm like,

2687
01:34:11.780 --> 01:34:13.517
is it even legal to drive that thing on the road?

2688
01:34:13.517 --> 01:34:15.280
You know, like stuff like that.

2689
01:34:15.280 --> 01:34:17.140
So I'm sure it'll blow your mind.

2690
01:34:17.140 --> 01:34:18.520
But I'm just curious

2691
01:34:18.520 --> 01:34:20.360
like what that conversation's gonna be like. (laughs)

2692
01:34:20.360 --> 01:34:22.120
We'll see. We'll see.

2693
01:34:22.120 --> 01:34:24.100
I think I might be taking him climbing too or something.

2694
01:34:24.100 --> 01:34:25.520
So we'll just see-
Oh, there you go.

2695
01:34:25.520 --> 01:34:26.788
How it all plays out.
Right on.

2696
01:34:26.788 --> 01:34:28.120
Yeah, I'm psyched.

2697
01:34:28.120 --> 01:34:29.604
Out in Burbank somewhere.

2698
01:34:29.604 --> 01:34:31.620
Or in a climbing gym?

2699
01:34:31.620 --> 01:34:32.550
I think Stoney Point.

2700
01:34:32.550 --> 01:34:35.050
It's kind of historic climbing area like north of here.

2701
01:34:35.050 --> 01:34:37.146
Oh, that's cool.
But we'll see.

2702
01:34:37.146 --> 01:34:39.530
I don't know, like all things in my life,

2703
01:34:39.530 --> 01:34:40.900
I'm like, I don't really know.

2704
01:34:40.900 --> 01:34:42.570
I'll just show up, see how it goes.

2705
01:34:42.570 --> 01:34:43.510
I'll just have a good time.

2706
01:34:43.510 --> 01:34:45.717
Alex, you have a say in these things.

2707
01:34:45.717 --> 01:34:47.000
Maybe.

2708
01:34:47.000 --> 01:34:48.670
It's easier not to.

2709
01:34:48.670 --> 01:34:50.460
Honestly, my whole life,

2710
01:34:50.460 --> 01:34:53.556
it's like easier to just go with the flow with it.

2711
01:34:53.556 --> 01:34:54.787
And you're like, "Cool, it's all a crazy adventure.

2712
01:34:54.787 --> 01:34:57.307
"I'm just on the ride and see how it plays out."

2713
01:34:57.307 --> 01:35:00.130
During the whole "Free Solo" craziness

2714
01:35:00.130 --> 01:35:02.907
did you ever max out and just be like, "I can't,

2715
01:35:02.907 --> 01:35:04.047
"I need like a break,

2716
01:35:04.047 --> 01:35:04.880
"I gotta get out of here"?

2717
01:35:04.880 --> 01:35:06.760
I think I skipped one event,

2718
01:35:06.760 --> 01:35:08.770
like in the whole "Free Solo" film tour,

2719
01:35:08.770 --> 01:35:10.220
which was like six or eight months

2720
01:35:10.220 --> 01:35:12.280
of like nonstop scheduling,

2721
01:35:12.280 --> 01:35:14.900
sometimes two events in two different cities

2722
01:35:14.900 --> 01:35:15.733
in the same day,

2723
01:35:15.733 --> 01:35:17.370
like something in Chicago and something in SF

2724
01:35:17.370 --> 01:35:19.350
in the same day, which is like pretty rugged

2725
01:35:19.350 --> 01:35:21.343
when you add the flights in between everything.

2726
01:35:21.343 --> 01:35:24.730
I think in the whole tour, there was one event

2727
01:35:24.730 --> 01:35:27.500
that I was supposed to go to that I was just like,

2728
01:35:27.500 --> 01:35:30.450
"I just can't" and had to go into the climbing gym instead.

2729
01:35:30.450 --> 01:35:34.740
And it was only because either Jimmy or Chai,

2730
01:35:34.740 --> 01:35:36.930
the co-directors took it over for me.

2731
01:35:36.930 --> 01:35:39.110
They did the event for me that night or something.

2732
01:35:39.110 --> 01:35:40.968
But, you know, I felt like it was pretty solid.

2733
01:35:40.968 --> 01:35:41.801
But then funny enough-

2734
01:35:41.801 --> 01:35:43.810
You show up with a smile on your face.

2735
01:35:43.810 --> 01:35:45.620
I mean, just from what I saw,

2736
01:35:45.620 --> 01:35:50.030
you completely were 100% present for it.

2737
01:35:50.030 --> 01:35:50.863
Well, that's the thing.

2738
01:35:50.863 --> 01:35:53.070
'Cause I knew it was like a once-in-a-lifetime adventure.

2739
01:35:53.070 --> 01:35:55.070
You know, instead of looking at it

2740
01:35:55.070 --> 01:35:56.990
as like this heinous work experience,

2741
01:35:56.990 --> 01:35:57.823
if you look at it as like,

2742
01:35:57.823 --> 01:36:00.870
this is a crazy adventure that I'll tell my grandkids about,

2743
01:36:00.870 --> 01:36:03.528
like the one time I got to sample the movie star life,

2744
01:36:03.528 --> 01:36:06.430
then it makes it not that bad because...

2745
01:36:07.520 --> 01:36:09.870
I kept calling it my deployment to Hollywood.

2746
01:36:09.870 --> 01:36:10.820
'Cause I think that people being

2747
01:36:10.820 --> 01:36:13.180
like deployed overseas for six months.

2748
01:36:13.180 --> 01:36:15.153
And it did kind of feel like that,

2749
01:36:15.153 --> 01:36:16.540
because it was totally different world,

2750
01:36:16.540 --> 01:36:17.830
totally different scene.

2751
01:36:17.830 --> 01:36:19.810
You know, I'm being taken places by car service.

2752
01:36:19.810 --> 01:36:21.750
And I'm like, "I don't use a car service."

2753
01:36:21.750 --> 01:36:24.700
Like I'm not used to having like a fancy SUV

2754
01:36:24.700 --> 01:36:25.930
sitting out front waiting for me

2755
01:36:25.930 --> 01:36:27.290
to like whisk me to a hotel,

2756
01:36:27.290 --> 01:36:28.760
but I'm just like, "I'm just going with it."

2757
01:36:28.760 --> 01:36:32.712
I'm doing what Brad does, Brad Pitt or whatever.

2758
01:36:32.712 --> 01:36:37.080
I loved how North Face made a tuxedo for you,

2759
01:36:37.080 --> 01:36:38.319
which is pretty cool.

2760
01:36:38.319 --> 01:36:39.870
Is that where you got married,

2761
01:36:39.870 --> 01:36:40.703
did you get married in the suit and tux?

2762
01:36:40.703 --> 01:36:42.230
I did, yeah, same tux.

2763
01:36:42.230 --> 01:36:43.063
Yeah, classy.

2764
01:36:43.063 --> 01:36:45.280
And funny enough, the formal wear stuff like that

2765
01:36:45.280 --> 01:36:46.470
actually does get more comfortable

2766
01:36:46.470 --> 01:36:47.303
if you wear it more.

2767
01:36:47.303 --> 01:36:49.573
Because I've only ever worn formal wear like once.

2768
01:36:49.573 --> 01:36:52.277
And you're like, "Oh, it's so starchy and painful.

2769
01:36:52.277 --> 01:36:53.930
"and not that nice."

2770
01:36:53.930 --> 01:36:55.530
And then if you wear them a couple of times,

2771
01:36:55.530 --> 01:36:56.363
you're actually like,

2772
01:36:56.363 --> 01:36:57.645
"Yeah, it starts to break in a little."

2773
01:36:57.645 --> 01:36:58.850
I'm breaking out the tux like once a week.

2774
01:36:58.850 --> 01:37:00.970
Yeah, totally.
For the deployment.

2775
01:37:00.970 --> 01:37:05.970
Dude, at the Oscars, the tux was like so tight and crisp

2776
01:37:06.160 --> 01:37:07.919
that I felt like my nipples chafed

2777
01:37:07.919 --> 01:37:10.040
like in the way that you read about that

2778
01:37:10.040 --> 01:37:11.310
like with the marathon runners,

2779
01:37:11.310 --> 01:37:13.083
like if you're running 20 miles in the rain or whatever

2780
01:37:13.083 --> 01:37:15.057
that you start chafing in weird ways.

2781
01:37:15.057 --> 01:37:16.920
You had an injury at the Oscars.

2782
01:37:16.920 --> 01:37:17.903
Totally.

2783
01:37:17.903 --> 01:37:18.736
I was like, "Oh," like I feel

2784
01:37:18.736 --> 01:37:21.960
like I sandpapered my nipples with this like starched shirt.

2785
01:37:21.960 --> 01:37:23.120
It was like, this is heinous.

2786
01:37:23.120 --> 01:37:26.840
I'm sure you had some surreal conversations with people.

2787
01:37:26.840 --> 01:37:27.673
Yeah, I know.

2788
01:37:28.975 --> 01:37:30.780
Yeah, Sanni too,

2789
01:37:30.780 --> 01:37:34.170
like my now wife had a, who was it?

2790
01:37:34.170 --> 01:37:36.010
He won best supporting actor I think

2791
01:37:36.010 --> 01:37:40.240
for like "Green Book" or I forget his name.

2792
01:37:40.240 --> 01:37:42.660
Viggo Mortensen.
No, his costar.

2793
01:37:42.660 --> 01:37:44.290
Mahershala Ali.
Yeah, Mahershala Ali.

2794
01:37:44.290 --> 01:37:45.251
Yeah, Mahershala Ali.

2795
01:37:45.251 --> 01:37:49.440
He was like walking by and basically like said hi to me

2796
01:37:49.440 --> 01:37:51.340
and then was like turning to introduce himself

2797
01:37:51.340 --> 01:37:52.470
to Sanni or something.

2798
01:37:52.470 --> 01:37:55.230
And she was like holding little like appetizer pizzas

2799
01:37:55.230 --> 01:37:57.060
in both hands and fully just threw it on the floor

2800
01:37:57.060 --> 01:37:58.813
and was like, "So great to meet you."

2801
01:37:58.813 --> 01:38:01.468
Had this really nice moment with him.

2802
01:38:01.468 --> 01:38:03.280
She was like so charmed.

2803
01:38:03.280 --> 01:38:04.250
And then like he wandered on,

2804
01:38:04.250 --> 01:38:06.630
he had this whole entourage with like a crew.

2805
01:38:06.630 --> 01:38:07.830
And then as soon as he walked away,

2806
01:38:07.830 --> 01:38:10.159
Sanni's like cleaning up her pizza off the floor.

2807
01:38:10.159 --> 01:38:12.497
You know, but it was like, "This is my chance

2808
01:38:12.497 --> 01:38:13.830
"to meet Mahershala Ali."

2809
01:38:13.830 --> 01:38:15.827
So it's like just like food on the floor and "Hello,

2810
01:38:15.827 --> 01:38:16.700
"so great to meet you."

2811
01:38:16.700 --> 01:38:17.810
Just things like that,

2812
01:38:17.810 --> 01:38:20.146
it's just fun.
I love that,

2813
01:38:20.146 --> 01:38:22.130
well, the story that I heard

2814
01:38:22.130 --> 01:38:25.356
and maybe it's apocryphal was that Jason Mamoa

2815
01:38:25.356 --> 01:38:28.680
was gonna be giving out the award, but it was like,

2816
01:38:28.680 --> 01:38:31.340
he wanted to be, if "Free Solo" was gonna win,

2817
01:38:31.340 --> 01:38:33.250
then he wanted to be the one to like say it.

2818
01:38:33.250 --> 01:38:35.753
Yeah, he's a big climber.
He's a climber buddy

2819
01:38:35.753 --> 01:38:36.634
and all that.

2820
01:38:36.634 --> 01:38:37.467
Yeah, he's a serious climber.

2821
01:38:37.467 --> 01:38:38.940
So it was pretty cool that he was the one who-

2822
01:38:38.940 --> 01:38:42.340
Yeah, he was genuinely like, gave everybody big hugs

2823
01:38:42.340 --> 01:38:43.173
and super psyched.

2824
01:38:43.173 --> 01:38:44.300
He seems like a good dude.

2825
01:38:44.300 --> 01:38:45.870
Dude, yeah.

2826
01:38:45.870 --> 01:38:47.480
I mean, you know, I only met him briefly

2827
01:38:47.480 --> 01:38:48.840
through the tour, but super nice guy.

2828
